    ["src/games/updates/85211729168715.txt"] = [[
0.1.44 | Steal An Exu: the steal now uses the working mechanism and takes MANY Exus, not one. The prompt is stolen by zeroing its HoldDuration and then firing InputHoldBegin + InputHoldEnd -- with HoldDuration = 0 the hold completes instantly and the server accepts the steal (this is what the pen actually did; the plain fire and the full-duration hold did not). The original HoldDuration is restored right after, so nothing is left changed on the game prompt. And the sweep no longer goes Nest by Nest: it walks every Exu in workspace.Runtime, one at a time, teleporting onto each one's Root and returning to your base between them -- so every Exu on the map is taken in the pass, instead of the same one over and over. Same for the Auto Steal Exu toggle and the Steal All button. The Terreiro dropdown is gone (the sweep covers all Exus, so picking a Terreiro no longer means anything). The result line is "4) roubo -> true".
0.1.43 | Steal An Exu: the steal now tries the correct prompt action. A spy on the prompt showed what the Dex actually does: it does not hold the prompt and does not call InputHoldBegin/End at all -- it fires the prompt directly, the same event the executor fire uses, and the Exu is taken. So the steal no longer relies on the hold timing: it tries InputHoldBegin on its own first (the route closest to what the Dex does), and only if the prompt does not answer does it fall back to the full hold and then to the plain fire. After the prompt answers, it waits a beat on top of the Exu before teleporting back, so the server can credit the steal. Same for the Auto Steal Exu toggle and the Steal All button, one after the other. The result line is "4) roubo -> true".
0.1.42 | Steal An Exu: the steal now finishes the hold before it leaves. The hold (InputHoldBegin -> HoldDuration -> InputHoldEnd) was being cut short by the teleport as soon as the Triggered event fired, and the Triggered was being used as the success check -- but it always fires, so it never meant the Exu was taken. Two changes: the Triggered is no longer the criterion (the completed hold is), and after the InputHoldEnd the script stays on top of the Exu for a short beat so the server can credit the steal before the teleport back to the base. The result line is now "4) hold(InputHoldBegin/End) -> true"; there is no more "5) Triggered" line. Same for the Auto Steal Exu toggle and the Steal All button. The fire fallback is still there for an executor without InputHold*.
0.1.41 | Steal An Exu: the steal now actually takes the Exu. Testing in game proved that fireproximityprompt does not steal here -- it fires the prompt locally but the server ignores it -- and that the game has no steal remote at all. What steals is HOLDING the prompt for its own HoldDuration: InputHoldBegin, wait 0.5s, InputHoldEnd. The steal step now holds the prompt that way (with the fire kept only as a fallback for an executor that lacks InputHold*), same for the Auto Steal Exu toggle and the Steal All button. Combined with the earlier fix (teleporting onto the Exu Root instead of the Nest), the steal now stands inside range and holds the prompt the way the game expects, so the Exu is credited. The Auto Upgrade Base and Auto Upgrade Esteira toggles are unchanged.
0.1.40 | Steal An Exu: the steal now teleports onto the Exu itself, and there are two new Auto Upgrade toggles. The steal bug was that the Nest is not always on top of the Exu -- the debug log showed distances of 14 to 24 studs on some Nests (the prompt only reaches 6.5), so the server was rejecting the fire even though the local trigger fired. The step now finds the exu in Runtime near the Nest, teleports ONTO its Root (inside range) instead of the Nest, then fires, then returns to the base to validate -- the same for both the Auto Steal Exu toggle and the Steal All button. Both are independently on the Steal An Exu tab. New section "Auto Upgrade" with two toggles: "Auto Upgrade Base" clicks the ClickDetector of your own base sign (PenSign.Board.ClickDetector) and "Auto Upgrade Esteira" clicks the treadmill sign (TreadSign.Board.ClickDetector). Both find YOUR base by the Owner attribute, so they follow you when the base changes, and both keep clicking while on. The debug lines now number the steps 1 to 6 and are still tagged [StealAnExu] in the console and the Debug tab.
0.1.39 | Steal An Exu slowed down and made talkative, because the exus were not getting stolen. The cycle now waits longer at every step: after the teleport onto an exu it lets the prompt settle before firing, after firing it waits longer for the prompt to trigger, and it holds on the base long enough for the game to confirm the stash (1.5s on the Auto Steal Exu toggle, 0.8s on Steal All). Every step prints a tagged line to the console and to the Debug tab -- "[StealAnExu] Nest3 2) prompt achado; dist= ... range= ...", "... 3c) Triggered (ativou) -> true/false" -- so it is visible whether the prompt is found, whether it is in range, whether fireproximityprompt ran, and whether the game actually triggered it. If a step is skipped, the reason is printed too.
0.1.38 | Steal An Exu no longer fights the map. The body floats in place (anchored, zeroed velocity) and clips through everything (noclip) while a run is active, so the teleport stops jamming you inside scenery -- collision and anchoring are put back exactly as they were when you stop. Both the Auto Steal Exu toggle and the Steal All button now use the same cycle: teleport onto the exu, wait for its prompt to be in range, fire it, wait for the prompt to trigger, teleport back to your base, then wait to let the game confirm the stash (1s on the toggle, shorter on Steal All) before moving to the next exu. Steal All is the faster, stronger pass (Terreiro9 to Terreiro1) and also tries the prompt from range before it blinks onto the nest.
0.1.37 | Steal An Exu gets a Steal All button (Farming > Auto Steal). One press clears EVERY exu in the game in a single pass, from Terreiro9 down to Terreiro1, with 0.25s between each. For each exu it tries the Runtime prompt from range first (the prompt's activation distance is widened for the instant it fires, then put back), and blinks the teleport onto the Nest for a second try so it still lands on a game that re-checks distance server-side. When the pass ends you are back exactly where you pressed it (your base). The Auto Steal Exu toggle is untouched and still loops one Terreiro on its own.
0.1.36 | Steal An Exu gets its first feature: Auto Steal Exu. Pick a Terreiro in the panel (Farming > Auto Steal), flip the toggle, and it clears that Terreiro in a loop -- an instant teleport to each Nest, uses the Runtime prompt on the exu that lives there, then back to your base to store it, 0.5s between rounds. Your base is found from the Owner attribute on the pens (matched to your own UserId) instead of a fixed Pen1, so it stays right after a server change and works for whoever runs the script. No position anti-cheat in the game, so the teleport is direct rather than a tween.
0.1.35 | Steal An Exu is recognised by the hub now. It has its own route (place 103076400317371 / universe 10771168447) so it no longer falls into the universal backend, and the backend is a clean skeleton with no features yet -- it loads, publishes its own state and is ready for whatever comes next. Nothing to toggle in the panel while the features are being built. Also: a reload at a game whose backend was added after the first list (Blood Strike, Steal An Egg, Violence District and now Steal An Exu) now tears the previous session down the same way every other backend does.
0.1.34 | The console is quiet again. The loader used to print a full startup report on every run: "subindo o hub", "executando o hub", a summary block with version / host / executor / backend, the sea count and "hub no ar". On a healthy load the only line now is "DepHub v<version>", printed by the hub itself. All that detail (host, executor, backend, island counts) stays in the Debug tab. A broken run is never silent: a failed download, a compile error, a hub that threw, a backend that did not publish state, or a Travel island list that came back empty still surface through warn.
0.1.33 | Two fixes in Blox Fruits. First, turning Grab fruit on or off no longer kills the rest of the fruit feature: the toggle used to call SetFeature("fruit", false), which runs the whole feature's Disable -- that also turned off Fruit ESP, stopped the tracer and cancelled auto store and auto random, so everything looked dead after touching Grab fruit. Grab now only drives its own switch, and the fruit feature stays alive while any of its switches is on (and reports itself enabled again when you re-arm one). Second, the multi-select dropdowns (Silent aim's exception keys, the Skip and Keep lists) now restore a saved selection for real: clearing a dropdown left the old selection behind inside the library, so it either kept showing the previous keys or went empty instead of the ones you saved. The library now clears the selection on Clear and exposes Select, and the adapter drives it on restore, so loading a config shows and applies exactly the keys you chose. The single dropdown (Island) was already going through the same open path -- the popup position fix from 0.1.32 is also in this build.
0.1.32 | Dropdowns open in the right place now. The dropdown popup is parented to the shell GUI outside the panel's UI scale, but its position was still being divided by that scale, so on a 1080p screen (scale ~1.2) a dropdown anchored at x=1157 opened at x=964 -- about 190px left of its own field and partly under the panel, which is why a dropdown could open already off its field or look like it never appeared at all. The position now uses raw screen coordinates on both axes, and the popup height no longer collapses when the shell reports a small height. The headless suite could not catch this before: its typeof double reported a table for an Instance, so the library treated the popup's parent as a property bag and built a popup with no children, and the visibility checks read a GetElements().Dropdowns folder that did not exist and were skipped without a word. The double now reports Instance like the real engine, and the library exposes the Dropdowns and Buttons folders through GetElements, so there are real render assertions: the popup is parented, visible, has a non-zero height and opens aligned with its field in raw pixels. Restoring a saved config to a multi-select dropdown (Silent aim's exception keys, the fruit Skip and Keep lists) also re-runs the feature callback now, so a loaded config actually re-applies the selection instead of only moving the field.
0.1.29 | Teleport stops for real now: turning a teleport toggle off cancels the flight in progress and the character stops where it is, and nothing re-flies until you re-arm it (the fruit grab used to keep dragging you to the last fruit, and a late island re-select could start a new flight). The loader no longer waits for the game or the character to load -- the hub opens instantly.
0.1.28 | The panel's dropdowns work now: a multi-select dropdown (Silent aim's exception keys, the fruit Skip and Keep lists) opened showing a bare ... and would not select anything. The adapter handed the library its default as a set table ({F = true}), but the library reads a multi default as an array and loops or i = 1, #Default -- so with a set it looped zero times, nothing was selected, and the field rendered its empty placeholder. The default is now passed as an array, so the dropdown opens with the saved keys already ticked and selecting one shows it in the field. A headless test now drives the real library (not just the stub): it builds a multi dropdown, proves the default is really selected, opens it (the popup becomes parented and visible), and closes it again, so the dropdown cannot silently regress again. Blox Fruits gains a Player ESP on the Player tab: highlight every other player and label them with their name, level and health, read the same way the OpenSource script reads them (the target's Data.Level and the Humanoid's Health/MaxHealth). A Player locator switch adds the exact distance to each player to the label, so you can see where everyone is, not just who. Both are saved with the rest of the panel. Blox Fruits also captures your own stats in the background and keeps them ready (no UI yet, store only): level, Beli, Fragments, race, current fruit and your equipped Sword, Gun and Melee, read through LP.Data.<field>.Value and the ToolTip scan of your character and backpack that the OpenSource script uses. And the Fruit ESP tracer stopped fighting the loaded library: the real library now drives the same open/close the tests exercise, so the popup can no longer be left visible outside its own dropdown.
0.1.27 | The panel is smaller, the icons are the library's own, and the open/close toggle is the library's. The window no longer grows with the screen: RedzLib scaled everything by ViewportSize.Y/450, so on a 1080p screen the panel was multiplied ~2.26x and a 600x420 window rendered at 1356x949 -- almost the whole screen. The scale now uses a fixed reference height (900) clamped to 0.75..1.25, so the panel renders at about its declared size (~1.13x, roughly 678x474 on 1080p) and stays the same size on any screen. Tab and category icons are now names looked up in the library's own icon catalogue (the real RedzLib Icons.lua, 818 names -> asset ids, vendored as src/core/redz-icons.lua) instead of emojis: ask for "swords", "apple" or "rocket" and you get the library's real icon; ask for a name the catalogue does not have and the tab simply has no icon, instead of showing a stray glyph. The open/close key (N) is now bound through the library's own Window:NewMinimizer instead of the shell's hand-rolled listener. The Travel island list is per sea: Sea 1, Sea 2 and Sea 3 have different maps, so the dropdown shows only the destinations of the sea the game reports (ReplicatedStorage.Util.Realm) -- Cafe on Sea 2, Castle on Sea on Sea 3 (also a fixed position), instead of mixing them. Also, the fruit tab drops the two distance sliders: fruit ESP and grab now have unlimited range, with no configurable limit, so a fruit is never ignored just because it is far -- the scan still only does the same cheap existence/validity checks. Skip list and Store keep list are multi-select fruit dropdowns built from the game's own fruit-name list: pick fruits by name, no typing, no duplicates, remove by unchecking, and the choices drive exactly the same skip/keep the old text fields did. Under the hood, three events that never worked now do: Heartbeat (island arrival), RenderStepped (dashboard FPS) and InputBegan (open/close key) were checked with type(x) == "table", but Roblox events are RBXScriptSignal, not tables, so the checks were always false and the connections never happened -- they are now pcall-guarded connects. And the panel now saves and reloads everything: two controls that were built with a hardcoded off and never registered (Blade hits and the Island teleport toggle) are now saved and applied like the rest, so a config written from the panel actually carries them. On top of that, the saved state reactivates itself for real on join -- a toggle saved on no longer just shows as on in the panel, it drives the feature (the fruit ESP saved on comes up with its draw loop running, the blade hit enabled, and so on), because the auto-load replays the same setter every control already uses on click. Auto execute is fixed too: the reload line could not find the executor's queue_on_teleport when it was only published on getgenv() (it read the bare global only, so the toggle silently did nothing on those executors), and the reload now always targets the same branch you launched instead of guessing. Under the hood the file-API, JSON and save/reload logic was pulled out of the Blox Fruits backend into one shared core module (src/core/config-store.lua) so the other games adopt the same persistence without copying it. The dropdown popup can no longer get stuck on the panel: the library reuses one dropdown popup for every dropdown in every tab, and its parent was left unbound by the library refactor, so it was created loose and framed at a fixed corner -- once anything attached it, it showed as a small dark box with a magnifier over the content (the box the user saw in the Combat tab under Blade hits). It is now born hidden inside the library's own overlay, only shown while a dropdown is actually open, and closed on every tab change, so it can never render outside its own dropdown. And the underlying state is now corrected across the board: the shared config store reads its file with a single guarded isfile call (the old code checked it twice and the second call ran outside the pcall, so an executor that throws there broke the whole Load), a Save now reads the file back and only reports success when the bytes match exactly what it wrote (a stale or partial file no longer counts as saved), and a Load now reports which settings actually applied versus which failed -- it no longer runs the same effect twice (the toggle callback fired once via the control and again via the direct setter) and no longer claims success when a feature refused the value. Teleport restores each part's original CanCollide instead of forcing everything collidable, so an accessory or decoration that was already non-collidable stays that way after a flight. The Player tab restores your character's original attribute values (and removes the ones that did not exist before) and destroys the Stun NumberValue only when the feature itself created it, instead of writing defaults over what the game had. Island teleport now checks whether the flight actually started -- a motor that returns nothing without error no longer leaves the toggle armed and reporting 'flying' with no movement -- and reports a real failure instead. The remote sniff toggle really stops capturing when switched off, even with the hooks already installed. And the auto-execute reload line now always targets the current session's ref (never a stale one left in shared state) and validates the ref and source URL before building the reload code, so a value another script wrote into the shared environment cannot shape the queued script. And the panel's small annoyances are cleaned up: the open/close key no longer fires while you are typing in chat or a text box (it only reacts to a real keyboard press that the game has not already handled), the remote sniffer only installs a hook when the executor gives it a way to remove it (otherwise it reports 'unsupported' and leaves the remote untouched, instead of leaving a hook it can never undo), removing the hub's own log hooks no longer overwrites a warn/print layer another script installed on top, the notes the hub keeps are now capped like its log buffer so a long session cannot grow them without limit, and the __DEPHUB_TOGGLE helper is removed when the panel closes if - and only if - it is still this session's, so a helper another session published is never wiped. And the lifecycle is now honest about failure: a feature whose Enable throws is turned back off and, when that call had just created it, dropped from the registry so the next toggle does not reuse it half-initialised; the runtime only marks itself started once every component has actually started, and a component that throws leaves it not-started with the reason recorded so a retry really runs instead of reporting a success that never happened; and disabling the silent aim only puts the __namecall hook back when the current hook is still its own, so a layer another script installed on top is never wiped -- it re-arms the metatable readonly state it had loosened and clears its own state either way. The saved config is now a real document, not a bare bag of keys: it carries a schema version, the game id and a timestamp, so a future format change has a number to migrate from and a config written by another game can never be applied here (it is refused, with the reason recorded). An older file -- saved before this format, with the values at the top level -- still loads exactly as before. The JSON is now read and written by the game own HttpService instead of a hand-rolled subset parser, so escaped quotes, backslashes, line breaks and accented characters survive a save/load round-trip instead of being silently mangled; the small built-in codec stays only as a fallback for an executor that does not expose HttpService. And a text input now exposes the same Set/SetValue the other controls do, so a saved value is pushed back into the field on screen as well as re-applied to the feature -- the handle drives the underlying textbox and updates its own value without firing the callback twice. The fruit tab also gains Auto random fruit: a toggle that rolls the fruit gacha for you and a Gacha cooldown slider for how long to wait between rolls. It calls the same remote the game does (the RF/GachaNetworkRF function, with a Purchase/ ZiolesGacha payload) and honors the cooldown the server returns when it sends one, falling back to the slider value otherwise; the roll stops the moment you turn the toggle off or close the panel. The Travel tab now lists the full island set for every sea, using the exact positions from the OpenSource script (16 islands on Sea 1, 14 on Sea 2, 15 on Sea 3, each sea showing only its own); the island teleport flies straight to those points and no longer depends on a map instance existing. The Player tab also gains Autoken and Autobuso: Autoken keeps Observation Haki on and Autobuso keeps Armament Haki on, both by watching the game flags (KenActive and BusoEnabled) and firing the haki remote only when the haki is actually off, instead of blindly spamming a key. The Blade hits section absorbs the auto-attack upgrades from the OpenSource script: it now takes the hit id automatically from a shared hook instead of needing it set by hand, it drops a fishing rod from your hand before swinging (equipped, the rod hijacks the attack and no hit lands), and it gains a Line of sight toggle that checks a raycast before hitting so it no longer hits enemies through walls -- the tell that gives an auto-hit away. Silent aim and Blade hits now share ONE __namecall hook (a single ref-counted layer that both subscribe to, installed by whichever needs it first and removed by the last) instead of each stacking its own, so there is one hook surface on the metatable, not two. Auto execute now stays off: the queued reload line reads the saved config before re-running, so it refuses to re-execute when the toggle is off, and the start clears any reload the executor still holds from a previous session -- the toggle no longer comes back on by itself after a server change. The bootstrap loader now waits for the game to finish loading and for your character to spawn (HumanoidRootPart plus a live Humanoid) before it runs the hub, so no feature acts before you have spawned properly; the wait is generic, works for every game, and never leaves you without the hub -- it warns and continues if the character is slow.
0.1.01 | Shared UI (every game): sections now hug their content. A section's height is the measured content of its body, and since that measurement already includes the body's own padding the old size added an extra 24px of dead space under the last control -- the empty band that showed up at the bottom of a section. Removed. Also added real VStack/HStack grouping: related controls sit side by side on one line (a toggle next to the slider that drives it, the switches of one feature together) across Volleyball 4.2, Universal, Violence District, TSB, MM2, Noob Piece, AOT:R, Blood Strike, Steal An Egg, Restaurant Tycoon 3 and Blox Fruits, so tabs are shorter and less cluttered. New shell primitives AddHStack/AddVStack, exposed as Kit.Row/Kit.Stack, with tests.
0.1.00 | Shared UI (every game) + Debug: full English pass. Every player-visible string -- tab names, section titles, toggle/slider labels, paragraphs, notifications, status reads and the Debug console vocabulary ([FAIL], module/probe details, command replies) -- is now English (was Portuguese). Docs and code comments stay in Portuguese. Also the default open/close key is now N. The Debug tab, loader notes and backend diagnostics all read in English.
0.0.99 | Blox Fruits: the shared UI kit is now loaded alongside the backend; tab organisation is unchanged (tabs are already purpose-named, and the sea category is kept on purpose because it scopes features to the sea the player is in).
0.0.85 | Blox Fruits: raid pacing, player ESP range, fly speed, persisted waypoints, legendary poll interval, extras summon wait, plus panel descriptions, feature search and automation status.
0.0.84 | Blox Fruits fruits: cap the fruit ESP distance, limit how far fruits are auto-collected, skip specific fruits on pickup, keep chosen fruits out of the auto-store, and set the chest cooldown.
0.0.83 | Blox Fruits config: delete, rename and reset a saved config, plus reset every control to its default or just one module at a time. The test harness now has an in-memory filesystem and JSON codec, so Save/Load is actually covered.
0.0.82 | Blox Fruits PVP: aimbot gains a max range and a body-part picker, the skill rotation follows a configurable order (it used to fire in random order every session), and Auto Bounty takes an ignore list.
0.0.81 | Blox Fruits farm: two real tuning sliders (Approach distance and Attack interval) that write straight through to the farm config. A slider with a fractional range now keeps its precision instead of rounding every value to a whole number, so an interval like 0.35s is actually reachable.
0.0.80 | Blox Fruits: the sidebar now shows only the sea you are in plus the shared pages. On Sea 2 that means GENERAL and SEA 2 -- the combined SEA 1/2 and SEA 2/3 categories are gone. Features shared by more than one sea (Travel on Sea 1/2, Events and Fruits on Sea 2/3) are re-filed under the current sea, so nothing disappears; the sea categories also got their own icons.
0.0.79 | Steal An Egg (place 107778070777162): teleport ported from Blox Fruits -- a CFrame tween fly that glides the HumanoidRootPart to the target at a set fly speed, zeroing linear velocity and stripping collision during the move (noclip) while never anchoring the character. Targets are saved in game as waypoints (persisted to disk when the executor exposes file IO, otherwise kept in memory), because the map coordinates are not known ahead of time. Controls live in a fixed Teleport tab.
0.0.78 | New backend for Steal An Egg (place 107778070777162): the walk-speed feature ported from the Blox Fruits player module, and only that -- a "Speed boost" toggle and a "Walk speed" slider (16-300, default 50) driven by a RenderStepped loop that re-applies the value every frame, so the game controller cannot overwrite it. Controls live in a fixed Speed tab.
0.0.77 | New backend for Blood Strike (place 114234929420007): a character ESP that reads fighters from workspace.Characters (Models named after their owner) with a fallback to the standard Players surface, plus a team check, range, and name/distance/health/chams options. Controls live in a fixed ESP tab.
0.0.76 | Every game now gets a fixed Dashboard tab built by the loader itself, so the same home screen shows no matter which backend runs: the player greeting card (avatar, name, clock) plus the game/server card, and live performance metrics (framerate, latency, players, session). It reuses the library's own dashboard builders, so there is no game-specific code.
0.0.75 | The shared UI theme was split into ten ordered modules under src/core/ui/theme/ (data, sub-tabs, constructor, theme-apply, notifications, window, extras, public-api, controls, lifecycle); the orchestrator loads them in the original section order and each adds its methods to the one Library table, so behaviour is unchanged while the 13k-line file is now maintainable in pieces.
0.0.74 | The real UI theme is now covered by an automated test: the suite loads the actual demon-theme.luau (not the stub the backend tests use), builds a window against a Roblox double, then creates a category, tab, section and controls and destroys it, so a theme regression fails the suite instead of only showing up in-game.
0.0.73 | The Debug tab and the print/warn log hooks are now opt-in and off by default, so the hub runs clean in every game; the collector and every backend probe keep running underneath. Set env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = true before loading, call __DEPHUB_DEBUG() live, or use the debug on|off console command to bring the tab back without reloading.
0.0.72 | The universal backend now builds its own UI in the shared shell (Movement, Visuals, Combate, Social, Servidor, Status) and instruments the Debug tab with its own probes, features and commands, so an unmapped game gets working controls and live diagnostics instead of an empty window.
0.0.71 | The loader now self-heals a stale bootstrap: when the remote version is newer than the cached build the executor served, it re-fetches DepHub.lua with a cache-bust token and re-executes it once, guarded against looping, so a fixed bootstrap URL never runs an old loader again.
0.0.70 | An unrecognised game now loads the universal backend automatically instead of stopping on the empty unsupported state; set env.__DEPHUB_DISABLE_UNIVERSAL to true to opt out and get the plain unsupported window back.
0.0.69 | The FPS metric now resolves through three routes in order -- FrameRate:GetValue(), FrameRate.Value, then the engine physics frame rate -- so it no longer sits at 0 on executors where FrameStats.FrameRate is exposed as a value object rather than a plain number.
0.0.68 | Module fetches now carry a per-session cache-bust token, so an executor's HttpGet cache can never serve a stale older revision of diagnostics.lua or any backend after main is updated. Also fixes the remote sniffer raising on an absent method and the Debug metrics crash when Stats members are missing.
0.0.67 | Remote sniffer no longer reads absent methods as raw indexes: a RemoteFunction has no FireServer and a RemoteEvent has no InvokeServer, so each method read is pcall-guarded and the absent one is skipped instead of raising and failing the whole backend load.
0.0.66 | Debug console metrics no longer crash when the executor does not expose Stats.FrameStats/Network: every optional Stats and Workspace read now resolves through FindFirstChild, so FPS/ping/titans degrade to no-data instead of raising.
0.0.65 | Every game backend now reports its own structure into the Debug tab: named instances, live feature state, watches and console commands, so a game update that breaks a dependency shows up as [FALHA] at the point of failure.
0.0.64 | Live Debug console: streaming log, live FPS/ping/memory/uptime metrics, feature state auto-refresh, warn/print/error hooks, remote sniffer and terminal commands in every game.
0.0.63 | Shared Debug tab: module health, game-structure probes, live feature state and a copyable diagnostic report in every game.
0.0.2 | Universal UI, lifecycle hardening and diagnostics
]],
