0.1.35 | Steal An Exu is recognised by the hub now. It has its own route (place 103076400317371 / universe 10771168447) so it no longer falls into the universal backend, and the backend is a clean skeleton with no features yet -- it loads, publishes its own state and is ready for whatever comes next. Nothing to toggle in the panel while the features are being built. Also: a reload at a game whose backend was added after the first list (Blood Strike, Steal An Egg, Violence District and now Steal An Exu) now tears the previous session down the same way every other backend does.
0.1.34 | The console is quiet again. The loader used to print a full startup report on every run: "subindo o hub", "executando o hub", a summary block with version / host / executor / backend, the sea count and "hub no ar". On a healthy load the only line now is "DepHub v<version>", printed by the hub itself. All that detail (host, executor, backend, island counts) stays in the Debug tab. A broken run is never silent: a failed download, a compile error, a hub that threw, a backend that did not publish state, or a Travel island list that came back empty still surface through warn.
0.1.33 | Two fixes in Blox Fruits. First, turning Grab fruit on or off no longer kills the rest of the fruit feature: the toggle used to call SetFeature("fruit", false), which runs the whole feature's Disable -- that also turned off Fruit ESP, stopped the tracer and cancelled auto store and auto random, so everything looked dead after touching Grab fruit. Grab now only drives its own switch, and the fruit feature stays alive while any of its switches is on (and reports itself enabled again when you re-arm one). Second, the multi-select dropdowns (Silent aim's exception keys, the Skip and Keep lists) now restore a saved selection for real: clearing a dropdown left the old selection behind inside the library, so it either kept showing the previous keys or went empty instead of the ones you saved. The library now clears the selection on Clear and exposes Select, and the adapter drives it on restore, so loading a config shows and applies exactly the keys you chose. The single dropdown (Island) was already going through the same open path -- the popup position fix from 0.1.32 is also in this build.
0.1.32 | Dropdowns open in the right place now. The dropdown popup is parented to the shell GUI outside the panel's UI scale, but its position was still being divided by that scale, so on a 1080p screen (scale ~1.2) a dropdown anchored at x=1157 opened at x=964 -- about 190px left of its own field and partly under the panel, which is why a dropdown could open already off its field or look like it never appeared at all. The position now uses raw screen coordinates on both axes, and the popup height no longer collapses when the shell reports a small height. The headless suite could not catch this before: its typeof double reported a table for an Instance, so the library treated the popup's parent as a property bag and built a popup with no children, and the visibility checks read a GetElements().Dropdowns folder that did not exist and were skipped without a word. The double now reports Instance like the real engine, and the library exposes the Dropdowns and Buttons folders through GetElements, so there are real render assertions: the popup is parented, visible, has a non-zero height and opens aligned with its field in raw pixels. Restoring a saved config to a multi-select dropdown (Silent aim's exception keys, the fruit Skip and Keep lists) also re-runs the feature callback now, so a loaded config actually re-applies the selection instead of only moving the field.
0.1.29 | Teleport stops for real now: turning a teleport toggle off cancels the flight in progress and the character stops where it is, and nothing re-flies until you re-arm it (the fruit grab used to keep dragging you to the last fruit, and a late island re-select could start a new flight). The loader no longer waits for the game or the character to load -- the hub opens instantly.
0.1.28 | The panel's dropdowns work now: a multi-select dropdown (Silent aim's exception keys, the fruit Skip and Keep lists) opened showing a bare ... and would not select anything. The adapter handed the library its default as a set table ({F = true}), but the library reads a multi default as an array and loops or i = 1, #Default -- so with a set it looped zero times, nothing was selected, and the field rendered its empty placeholder. The default is now passed as an array, so the dropdown opens with the saved keys already ticked and selecting one shows it in the field. A headless test now drives the real library (not just the stub): it builds a multi dropdown, proves the default is really selected, opens it (the popup becomes parented and visible), and closes it again, so the dropdown cannot silently regress again. Blox Fruits gains a Player ESP on the Player tab: highlight every other player and label them with their name, level and health, read the same way the OpenSource script reads them (the target's Data.Level and the Humanoid's Health/MaxHealth). A Player locator switch adds the exact distance to each player to the label, so you can see where everyone is, not just who. Both are saved with the rest of the panel. Blox Fruits also captures your own stats in the background and keeps them ready (no UI yet, store only): level, Beli, Fragments, race, current fruit and your equipped Sword, Gun and Melee, read through LP.Data.<field>.Value and the ToolTip scan of your character and backpack that the OpenSource script uses. And the Fruit ESP tracer stopped fighting the loaded library: the real library now drives the same open/close the tests exercise, so the popup can no longer be left visible outside its own dropdown.
0.1.27 | The panel is smaller, the icons are the library's own, and the open/close toggle is the library's. The window no longer grows with the screen: RedzLib scaled everything by ViewportSize.Y/450, so on a 1080p screen the panel was multiplied ~2.26x and a 600x420 window rendered at 1356x949 -- almost the whole screen. The scale now uses a fixed reference height (900) clamped to 0.75..1.25, so the panel renders at about its declared size (~1.13x, roughly 678x474 on 1080p) and stays the same size on any screen. Tab and category icons are now names looked up in the library's own icon catalogue (the real RedzLib Icons.lua, 818 names -> asset ids, vendored as src/core/redz-icons.lua) instead of emojis: ask for "swords", "apple" or "rocket" and you get the library's real icon; ask for a name the catalogue does not have and the tab simply has no icon, instead of showing a stray glyph. The open/close key (N) is now bound through the library's own Window:NewMinimizer instead of the shell's hand-rolled listener. Blox Fruits Travel gains a Cafe destination: it is a position, not a map instance, so picking Cafe flies straight to the Cafe coordinates (no instance lookup, no rotation applied). The Travel island list is now per sea: Sea 1, Sea 2 and Sea 3 have different maps, so the dropdown shows only the destinations of the sea the game reports (ReplicatedStorage.Util.Realm) -- Cafe on Sea 2, Castle on Sea on Sea 3 (also a fixed position), instead of mixing them. Blox Fruits gains Silent aim on the Combat tab, ported from the script the user was building separately: it picks the nearest player in range and rewrites the position argument of every outgoing remote, so the server believes you aimed at them without the camera moving. It is one toggle, a multi-select dropdown of the keys that release the aim (Z X C V F, F by default -- hold any of them and the aim lets go), and a Range slider (500 to 1000, default 500). A beam (the original's dark purple, camera-facing) draws from your character to the current target so you can see who the aim is on -- it appears only while there is a target and no exception key is held, and it is created on enable and destroyed on disable together with the hook. The hook is installed on enable and fully removed on disable, so the game never keeps it dangling; if the executor does not expose the metatable primitives the toggle refuses instead of turning on a no-op. Silent aim also stops fighting the camera: the hook rewrites the position argument only when that argument really is a position (a Vector3 or CFrame), never a boolean or anything else, so an unrelated call -- the camera brushing a part, a character call -- passes through untouched instead of being rewritten with the target's coordinates, which was what pushed the player off their own character while the aim was on. Blox Fruits also gains a Player tab, for your own character only. It sets the character's own attributes: a Speed multiplier slider (the game's default is 2.3), an Unbreakable toggle (sets UnbreakableAll true and keeps it true if the game tries to clear it), a Water walk toggle (WaterWalking), and a Flashstep cooldown slider (0 = always ready, 1 = normal cooldown). Stun is kept at 0 -- it is a NumberValue the game puts on your character, and 1 is what stuns you, so the feature rewrites it back to 0 whenever the game sets it. Everything rebinds when you respawn, and disabling puts the character back to the game's own values. The game's skill cooldowns are left exactly as they are. Silent aim also holds its target hard now: once it is locked on a player it stays there -- a nearby player who is only a little closer no longer pulls the aim away, and a frame where the target's character is gone (death, respawn, dash) no longer drops the lock. It keeps the same target across that instant instead of jumping to someone else, which is what made it feel like the aim kept leaving the fight; it only switches when the current target is really gone or another player is much closer. Silent aim now covers the click too: the game's click attack (left mouse / auto-click) goes out as RegisterHit with the target part in slot 1, not a position in slot 2 like the keyboard skills, so the aim now redirects that part to the locked target's HumanoidRootPart and leaves the other slots (the hit id) untouched. Click and skills both land on the target now. Silent aim also gained a Debug logs switch in its section: turn it on and every attack that goes out prints, in the console, the remote name and the type of each argument it carries -- so if an attack still does not take the target, the print shows exactly what that attack sends instead of guessing. The click's slot index was corrected to where the game actually puts the part (the first argument of the call is the remote itself). Also, the fruit tab drops the two distance sliders: fruit ESP and grab now have unlimited range, with no configurable limit, so a fruit is never ignored just because it is far -- the scan still only does the same cheap existence/validity checks. Skip list and Store keep list are multi-select fruit dropdowns built from the game's own fruit-name list: pick fruits by name, no typing, no duplicates, remove by unchecking, and the choices drive exactly the same skip/keep the old text fields did. Under the hood, three events that never worked now do: Heartbeat (island arrival), RenderStepped (dashboard FPS) and InputBegan (open/close key) were checked with `type(x) == "table"`, but Roblox events are RBXScriptSignal, not tables, so the checks were always false and the connections never happened -- they are now pcall-guarded connects. Blox Fruits also gains silent aim click support: the left-click/mouse attack, which the game sends through its RegisterHit remote with the target part in the argument after the remote itself, is redirected to the locked target's HumanoidRootPart the same way the keyboard skills are -- no target, the click goes out untouched. A Debug logs switch on the Silent aim section prints, in the console, the name and argument types of every outgoing attack remote (rate-limited per remote), so if an attack still does not take the target the print shows what that attack actually sends on the user's executor instead of guessing. And the panel config is now saved and loaded: the commands in every control are recorded, Save config writes them to DepHub_BloxFruits_config.json through the executor's file API (kept in memory when the executor has none) and Load config puts them back, applying to both the live state and the on-screen controls. The config is also loaded automatically on start, so a saved setup comes back without pressing anything. Silent aim now lands the hit instead of only aiming: the debug prints showed the natural click does not send the game's RegisterHit from the client on this build, and rewriting the position argument of the skill remotes alone does not guarantee the hit -- so when the game opens a swing (RegisterAttack), the aim fires a RegisterHit directly at the locked target's HumanoidRootPart, which is the path that actually registers damage (the same one the blade hit uses). It reuses the hitId the game already sent (or generates one in the same format when none was seen yet), fires it out of band so it does not re-enter the hook, and caps it at one forged hit per swing. The debug print now shows the real remote name, the Instance name in the part slot and the actual Vector3 coordinates, so an attack can be identified instead of showing just "RemoteEvent". The CONFIG section gains an Auto execute toggle: turned on, the hub re-runs itself after the next teleport through the executor's queue_on_teleport, so the script comes back up without pasting it again; it is saved like the rest, so a saved on re-queues on the next start, and an executor without queue_on_teleport says so instead of pretending it turned on. The fruit ESP gets a clearer look and a tracer: the highlight is a brighter fill with a white outline drawn always-on-top so a fruit stands out from the ground, the label shows the fruit name and the distance in the fruit's own yellow, and a new Fruit tracer toggle -- separate from Fruit ESP, since highlight and distance still come with the ESP -- draws a yellow beam from your character to each fruit, created per fruit on enable and destroyed on disable. The Fruit tracer choice is saved with the rest of the panel. The silent aim Debug logs switch and its console prints are gone, since they did their job and the natural click path is now known. The island teleport toggle now really stops the teleport: turning it off (or anything tied to it) cancels the flight in progress -- the motor's tween is cancelled so the character stops being dragged, the arrival watcher is disarmed, and collision is restored -- instead of only refusing to start new flights and letting the current one finish. The teleport motor is lighter per frame: the noclip pass no longer walks Character:GetDescendants() every Heartbeat (the heavy part of the flight loop); the BasePart list is cached and rebuilt only when the character changes (respawn), so the normal frame just zeroes velocity and flips CanCollide on a short cached array. Saving the panel config is also hardened: the file API is read from both the chunk globals and getgenv() (an executor that publishes only one used to fall back to memory in silence, which is the "it does not save" the user hit), the Save reads the file back after writing and only reports success when the bytes are really there, and a failed Save now names the real reason (no file API, writefile refused, or the file did not come back) instead of a generic "session only". Also, the script's own per-frame cost is lighter when the aim is on: the silent aim no longer allocates two closures every frame in its Heartbeat/RenderStepped handlers, and its retry to find the game's attack remotes is now bounded instead of running sixty times a second forever if a remote never shows up. And the panel now saves and reloads everything: two controls that were built with a hardcoded off and never registered (Blade hits and the Island teleport toggle) are now saved and applied like the rest, so a config written from the panel actually carries them. On top of that, the saved state reactivates itself for real on join -- a toggle saved on no longer just shows as on in the panel, it drives the feature (the fruit ESP saved on comes up with its draw loop running, the blade hit enabled, and so on), because the auto-load replays the same setter every control already uses on click. Auto execute is fixed too: the reload line could not find the executor's queue_on_teleport when it was only published on getgenv() (it read the bare global only, so the toggle silently did nothing on those executors), and the reload now always targets the same branch you launched instead of guessing. Under the hood the file-API, JSON and save/reload logic was pulled out of the Blox Fruits backend into one shared core module (src/core/config-store.lua) so the other games adopt the same persistence without copying it. The dropdown popup can no longer get stuck on the panel: the library reuses one dropdown popup for every dropdown in every tab, and its parent was left unbound by the library refactor, so it was created loose and framed at a fixed corner -- once anything attached it, it showed as a small dark box with a magnifier over the content (the box the user saw in the Combat tab under Blade hits). It is now born hidden inside the library's own overlay, only shown while a dropdown is actually open, and closed on every tab change, so it can never render outside its own dropdown. And the underlying state is now corrected across the board: the shared config store reads its file with a single guarded isfile call (the old code checked it twice and the second call ran outside the pcall, so an executor that throws there broke the whole Load), a Save now reads the file back and only reports success when the bytes match exactly what it wrote (a stale or partial file no longer counts as saved), and a Load now reports which settings actually applied versus which failed -- it no longer runs the same effect twice (the toggle callback fired once via the control and again via the direct setter) and no longer claims success when a feature refused the value. Teleport restores each part's original CanCollide instead of forcing everything collidable, so an accessory or decoration that was already non-collidable stays that way after a flight. The Player tab restores your character's original attribute values (and removes the ones that did not exist before) and destroys the Stun NumberValue only when the feature itself created it, instead of writing defaults over what the game had. Island teleport now checks whether the flight actually started -- a motor that returns nothing without error no longer leaves the toggle armed and reporting 'flying' with no movement -- and reports a real failure instead. The remote sniff toggle really stops capturing when switched off, even with the hooks already installed. And the auto-execute reload line now always targets the current session's ref (never a stale one left in shared state) and validates the ref and source URL before building the reload code, so a value another script wrote into the shared environment cannot shape the queued script. And the panel's small annoyances are cleaned up: the open/close key no longer fires while you are typing in chat or a text box (it only reacts to a real keyboard press that the game has not already handled), the remote sniffer only installs a hook when the executor gives it a way to remove it (otherwise it reports 'unsupported' and leaves the remote untouched, instead of leaving a hook it can never undo), removing the hub's own log hooks no longer overwrites a warn/print layer another script installed on top, the notes the hub keeps are now capped like its log buffer so a long session cannot grow them without limit, and the __DEPHUB_TOGGLE helper is removed when the panel closes if - and only if - it is still this session's, so a helper another session published is never wiped. And the lifecycle is now honest about failure: a feature whose Enable throws is turned back off and, when that call had just created it, dropped from the registry so the next toggle does not reuse it half-initialised; the runtime only marks itself started once every component has actually started, and a component that throws leaves it not-started with the reason recorded so a retry really runs instead of reporting a success that never happened; and disabling the silent aim only puts the __namecall hook back when the current hook is still its own, so a layer another script installed on top is never wiped -- it re-arms the metatable readonly state it had loosened and clears its own state either way. The saved config is now a real document, not a bare bag of keys: it carries a schema version, the game id and a timestamp, so a future format change has a number to migrate from and a config written by another game can never be applied here (it is refused, with the reason recorded). An older file -- saved before this format, with the values at the top level -- still loads exactly as before. The JSON is now read and written by the game own HttpService instead of a hand-rolled subset parser, so escaped quotes, backslashes, line breaks and accented characters survive a save/load round-trip instead of being silently mangled; the small built-in codec stays only as a fallback for an executor that does not expose HttpService. And a text input now exposes the same Set/SetValue the other controls do, so a saved value is pushed back into the field on screen as well as re-applied to the feature -- the handle drives the underlying textbox and updates its own value without firing the callback twice. The fruit tab also gains Auto random fruit: a toggle that rolls the fruit gacha for you and a Gacha cooldown slider for how long to wait between rolls. It calls the same remote the game does (the RF/GachaNetworkRF function, with a Purchase/ ZiolesGacha payload) and honors the cooldown the server returns when it sends one, falling back to the slider value otherwise; the roll stops the moment you turn the toggle off or close the panel. The Travel tab now lists the full island set for every sea, using the exact positions from the OpenSource script (16 islands on Sea 1, 14 on Sea 2, 15 on Sea 3, each sea showing only its own); the island teleport flies straight to those points and no longer depends on a map instance existing. The Player tab also gains Autoken and Autobuso: Autoken keeps Observation Haki on and Autobuso keeps Armament Haki on, both by watching the game flags (KenActive and BusoEnabled) and firing the haki remote only when the haki is actually off, instead of blindly spamming a key. The Blade hits section absorbs the auto-attack upgrades from the OpenSource script: it now takes the hit id automatically from a shared hook instead of needing it set by hand, it drops a fishing rod from your hand before swinging (equipped, the rod hijacks the attack and no hit lands), and it gains a Line of sight toggle that checks a raycast before hitting so it no longer hits enemies through walls -- the tell that gives an auto-hit away. Silent aim and Blade hits now share ONE __namecall hook (a single ref-counted layer that both subscribe to, installed by whichever needs it first and removed by the last) instead of each stacking its own, so there is one hook surface on the metatable, not two. Auto execute now stays off: the queued reload line reads the saved config before re-running, so it refuses to re-execute when the toggle is off, and the start clears any reload the executor still holds from a previous session -- the toggle no longer comes back on by itself after a server change. The bootstrap loader now waits for the game to finish loading and for your character to spawn (HumanoidRootPart plus a live Humanoid) before it runs the hub, so no feature acts before you have spawned properly; the wait is generic, works for every game, and never leaves you without the hub -- it warns and continues if the character is slow.
0.1.26 | The whole interface now renders on RedzLib, the vendored library in the repo (src/dephubv2lib.lua). The old theme was retired and the shared shell was rewritten as a thin adapter over RedzLib, so every game keeps the same tabs, sections and controls it had before -- look and behaviour did not change, only what draws them. Two things are visible: the open/close key is still N (the shell binds it itself, because RedzLib has no key binding of its own), and group rows now stack their controls vertically instead of side by side (RedzLib has no horizontal container; the controls are all still there, just one under the other). Blox Fruits island teleport also stops disarming by hand: once the character actually reaches the island you picked, that toggle turns itself off. It only flips on real arrival at that flight's destination (25 studs of the island), never starts early and never fires again after you have landed.
0.1.25 | Blox Fruits gains Island Teleport, a new Travel tab. Turn the toggle on, pick an island from the dropdown, and the character flies there. The toggle is a real gate: selecting an island while it is off only remembers the choice, and switching islands while it is on flies again. The destination is the real instance in the map (workspace.Map.<Name>) rather than a hardcoded coordinate, so it does not break when the map changes and it uses exactly the names the user confirmed; if the island is not in the map it says so instead of flying into empty space. The list is the Sea 2 set the user provided: Snow Mountain, Indra Island, Ice Castle, Green Bit, Grave Island, Ghost Ship Interior, Ghost Ship, Forgotten Island, Fishmen, Dressrosa, Darkbeard Arena and Dapper Jack Arena. It has no motor of its own -- it uses the shared teleport by injection, like the fruit feature does. No sliders.
0.1.24 | Blox Fruits fruit detection fixed for the fruits the game spawns. The feature only ever looked at Tools, but a fruit the game spawns into the map is a Model named Fruit/fruit with no Handle -- the Handle (and the ProximityPrompt) only appears once a player touches it. So a spawned fruit never got ESP and the grab never flew, while a fruit dropped by another player (which IS a Tool) worked -- exactly what the user reported. Detection now accepts both shapes: a Tool with OriginalName/Handle, and a Model (or Part) whose name is Fruit-like. ESP also stops orphaning its highlight: the fruit part can change while the fruit exists, and the old code reused the entry and left the adorner pointing at a dead part, which was the glitch the user saw; it now re-targets the adorner when the part changes. The grabbed spawned fruit is reached by flying onto it -- touching it is what makes the game turn the Model into a collectible Tool, so the grab does not refuse just because there is no prompt yet.
0.1.23 | Blox Fruits raid is parked. The raid feature was not behaving the way the user needs and he is going to rebuild it from scratch, so the whole thing was taken out of the active list rather than left half-working: src/games/features/bloxfruits/raid.lua moved to features/bloxfruits/parked/raid.lua (nothing deleted), the RAID section and both toggles (Auto raid, Auto complete raid) are gone from the Farm tab, and the raid is out of the manifest so it no longer loads, instantiates or bundles. The blade hit and the fruit feature are untouched. The backend keeps the combat injection slot in the feature context, reserved for whoever attacks through the shared blade hit next.
0.1.22 | Auto complete raid no longer picks islands by distance -- it walks them by number. The user checked the map with Dex and confirmed the islands are workspace.Map.RaidMap.RaidIsland1, RaidIsland2, RaidIsland3..., so the number IS the order, and "nearest" was the wrong idea: it could fly to an island that is close but out of sequence. Now it starts on island 1, stays while that island still has a live mob, advances to the next number only once it is clear, and when there is no next island AND no mob left alive it calls the round over: it stops acting and just waits, letting the game close the round on its own. It never restarts anything. Mobs are looked for in Workspace.Enemies and Workspace.Characters and inside the current island. Starting the toggle resets the sequence to island 1.
0.1.21 | Blox Fruits raid gets a second, narrower toggle: Auto complete raid. Where Auto raid buys the chip, flies to the tube and starts the raid, Auto complete finishes a raid that is already running -- and does nothing else. It reads the islands under workspace.Map.RaidMap, flies to the one nearest the player right now, lets the blade hit kill the mobs, then recalculates and moves to the next nearest, so it advances one island at a time instead of jumping around. No owner filter: it picks purely by distance, as the user specified. It holds off re-issuing the flight while already close (under 40 studs) so the character does not vibrate in place fighting the tween. The toggle turns the blade hit on when it reaches an island and turns it off again when switched off -- but only if the complete turned it on; a blade hit the player enabled stays enabled.
0.1.20 | Blox Fruits gains its auto raid, built on the two motors the script already has instead of new ones. features/bloxfruits/raid.lua runs the whole cycle the old events.lua did: it makes sure a Microchip is in hand (asking the raid NPC to buy the one for the chosen element when it is not), flies to the raid tube with the shared teleport motor, equips the chip again on arrival and fires the tube button through fireproximityprompt, and once the server says the raid started (the Raids remote's StartTimer event) it starts clearing waves -- by driving the blade hit it was handed, never by firing its own attack, so there is one attack motor in the script, not two. Injection just grew a second piece: every feature now gets {teleport, combat, state}, where combat is the blade hit when it exists; without a teleport the raid refuses outright, and without the blade hit it still enters but reports that it has nothing to attack with. The raid picks its tube by sea (ReplicatedStorage.Util.Realm, the same source the old script used), with the Microchip element selectable from a dropdown. Also: the fruit tab moved under the Farm category -- a fruit picker and a raid runner are both Farm, not two categories for one game.
0.1.19 | Blox Fruits gets its fruit feature, built on the shared teleport motor. features/bloxfruits/fruit.lua covers Grab, ESP and Store: it finds ground fruits (a Tool in Workspace carrying OriginalName and a Handle), highlights them with a distance label, flies to the nearest with the teleport motor and fires its ProximityPrompt, and stores held fruits through the CommF_ remote while respecting a never-store list. The point is the integration the user asked for -- the fruit does NOT roll its own teleport, it receives the motor as a context at creation (options.teleport) and calls tp:GoToInstance(handle); without a motor the ESP and store still work and only the grab refuses. Everything else the old fruits.lua mixed in (gacha, dealer, awakening, chests) stays out. The backend now builds its panel from a per-feature PANELS table (category, tab, section, controls) instead of hardcoding one feature, and passes each feature a context with the teleport motor.
0.1.18 | Blox Fruits gains its teleport motor, and the blade hit loses its sliders. features/bloxfruits/teleport.lua is the open-source fly, ported to the own-module shape with no UI and no toggle: it is infrastructure, not a panel feature. State:Teleport() publishes it to every feature (and the console) already enabled, so a fruit-picker can call tp:GoToInstance(part) or tp:GoToIsland("Jungle") to move the character -- one tested teleport instead of each feature re-implementing its own. Go tweens the HumanoidRootPart with duration scaled by distance, zeroes the linear velocity and noclips during the flight (never Anchors: BF rejects every hit on an anchored root), and carries the full island/boss tables for all three seas. GoToInstance accepts a BasePart or Model, which is the contract a world-scanning feature hands it. The blade hit now exposes only a toggle: range defaults to 300 and the interval to 0.05s -- the strongest that still works -- set on the module (SetRange/SetInterval remain for the console) but with no sliders in the panel.
0.1.17 | Blox Fruits keeps only the blade hit. Every other feature the user took out was moved to features/bloxfruits/parked/ with git mv -- nothing deleted, just out of the active folder -- and parked means parked: not loaded, not instantiated, not in the manifest, not in the bundle (bundle.js now skips that subtree). The global DISABLED flag and its __DEPHUB_BF_DISABLED test override are gone: there is no switch that turns the whole game off, what loads is exactly what is listed in FEATURE_MODULES, and a parked feature is rejected as an unknown name like any other. The backend also lost its now-meaningless parked state from State and GetStatus.
0.1.16 | Blox Fruits is now the user's own script, feature by feature. The backend no longer loads the vendored open-source tree (oss/): it loads one self-contained file per feature from features/bloxfruits/, each doing its own game:GetService and publishing new()/Enable()/Disable() with no DepHub dependency, and it only downloads the tree, builds the panel and flips the toggle. The blade hit comes back first as features/bloxfruits/bladhits.lua: it fires the game's own RegisterAttack/RegisterHit at every enemy in range on a jittered loop, resolves the remotes through Modules.Net with a ReplicatedStorage.Net fallback, refuses cleanly when they are absent, and carries the server-validated hitId as the second remote's 4th argument. Range and interval are set on the module itself (SetRange/SetInterval) rather than through a parallel config on the backend. The oss/ files stay on disk but are no longer tracked or bundled.
0.1.15 | Blox Fruits is turned off -- by request, and nothing was deleted. One master switch (DISABLED = true in src/games/bloxfruits.lua) parks the whole backend: no module initialises, so no feature registers a control, and the frontend is never built, so no tab, section, category or sea category from Blox Fruits appears in the panel. Every file stays committed and downloadable, the loader still routes the two place ids and the backend still returns its State, so the Debug tab reports "BloxFruits.Enabled = no" and explains how to turn it back on. Set DISABLED = false to restore the whole tree exactly as it was.
0.1.14 | Back to Blox Fruits: two features from the old script return. Blade hits auto-swings the equipped weapon at every enemy in range by firing the game's own attack remotes (RegisterAttack + RegisterHit) on a jittered cadence, with sliders for range and interval (ms) and switches for the Enemies/Characters folders and nearby players; the hit carries the server-validated hitId, which it now follows on every capture instead of reusing a stale one. Flashstep (no cooldown) keeps the character's FlashstepCooldown attribute pinned at zero so the move is always ready, and rebinds on respawn. Both sit in the panel (Blade hits in PVP, Flashstep in Player) and clean up fully on unload.
0.1.13 | Fix: the Impulse slider was invisible in Volleyball's Jump feature. A slider shares a line (HStack) with the toggle, and the shell squeezed it to half width -- but the slider's internals are right-anchored with fixed offsets, so at half width its label collapsed and the -/+ buttons overflowed off the edge. Two fixes: the Jump toggle and slider now each take their own full-width line, and the shell's row layout refuses to squeeze a slider (it keeps full width instead of sharing the line).
0.1.12 | Volleyball 4.2 gains its first feature since the restart: Jump Anywhere. One toggle turns on both the jump bypass (patches the game's CharacterActions.Jump check so it stops vetoing jumps) and the extra impulse that speeds the jump up; one slider sets the impulse strength (0-150%, 0% keeps the original jump). With no executor debug.getupvalue/setupvalue the bypass cannot apply, so the toggle reports it and the backend keeps running. It sits in a Movement tab and is fully restored on unload.
0.1.11 | Volleyball 4.2 restart: every feature was removed from the backend to start over. Gone: ball prediction, auto position, forced jump, ceiling noclip, Auto Set Aim, the fixed set parts, the whole tab UI and the backend's own diagnostics. What stays is the game-action automation -- the actions.lua module and State:Act(name, ...) -- so set (front/back), spike, block, bump, dive, spawn ball and serve remain available to whatever features come next.
0.1.10 | The loader is quiet on the console now: a healthy load prints exactly one line, "DepHub v<version>", instead of the startup chatter (executor, place, host, progress). That detail is still recorded in full for the Debug tab. Real failures (a backend that threw, an unknown place, a reload that did not take) still surface through warn so a broken run is never silent.
0.1.09 | Fixed the backend failing to load with "attempt to call missing method 'AddHStack'": the shared shell exposed the row/stack builders on the library object instead of the section handle the theme actually returns, so any CreateRow failed here. Row and stack grouping now goes through the section handle like every other control.
0.1.01 | Shared UI (every game): sections now hug their content. A section's height is the measured content of its body, and since that measurement already includes the body's own padding the old size added an extra 24px of dead space under the last control -- the empty band that showed up at the bottom of a section. Removed. Also added real VStack/HStack grouping: related controls sit side by side on one line (a toggle next to the slider that drives it, the switches of one feature together) across Volleyball 4.2, Universal, Violence District, TSB, MM2, Noob Piece, AOT:R, Blood Strike, Steal An Egg, Restaurant Tycoon 3 and Blox Fruits, so tabs are shorter and less cluttered. New shell primitives AddHStack/AddVStack, exposed as Kit.Row/Kit.Stack, with tests.
0.1.00 | Shared UI (every game) + Debug: full English pass. Every player-visible string -- tab names, section titles, toggle/slider labels, paragraphs, notifications, status reads and the Debug console vocabulary ([FAIL], module/probe details, command replies) -- is now English (was Portuguese). Docs and code comments stay in Portuguese. Also the default open/close key is now N. The Debug tab, loader notes and backend diagnostics all read in English.
0.0.99 | Blox Fruits: the shared UI kit is now loaded alongside the backend; tab organisation is unchanged (tabs are already purpose-named, and the sea category is kept on purpose because it scopes features to the sea the player is in).
0.0.85 | Blox Fruits: raid pacing, player ESP range, fly speed, persisted waypoints, legendary poll interval, extras summon wait, plus panel descriptions, feature search and automation status.
0.0.84 | Blox Fruits fruits: cap the fruit ESP distance, limit how far fruits are auto-collected, skip specific fruits on pickup, keep chosen fruits out of the auto-store, and set the chest cooldown.
0.0.83 | Blox Fruits config: delete, rename and reset a saved config, plus reset every control to its default or just one module at a time. The test harness now has an in-memory filesystem and JSON codec, so Save/Load is actually covered.
0.0.82 | Blox Fruits PVP: aimbot gains a max range and a body-part picker, the skill rotation follows a configurable order (it used to fire in random order every session), and Auto Bounty takes an ignore list.
0.0.81 | Blox Fruits farm: two real tuning sliders (Approach distance and Attack interval) that write straight through to the farm config. A slider with a fractional range now keeps its precision instead of rounding every value to a whole number, so an interval like 0.35s is actually reachable.
0.0.80 | Blox Fruits: the sidebar now shows only the sea you are in plus the shared pages. On Sea 2 that means GENERAL and SEA 2 -- the combined SEA 1/2 and SEA 2/3 categories are gone. Features shared by more than one sea (Travel on Sea 1/2, Events and Fruits on Sea 2/3) are re-filed under the current sea, so nothing disappears; the sea categories also got their own icons.
0.0.79 | Steal An Egg (place 107778070777162): teleport ported from Blox Fruits -- a CFrame tween fly that glides the HumanoidRootPart to the target at a set fly speed, zeroing linear velocity and stripping collision during the move (noclip) while never anchoring the character. Targets are saved in game as waypoints (persisted to disk when the executor exposes file IO, otherwise kept in memory), because the map coordinates are not known ahead of time. Controls live in a fixed Teleport tab.
0.0.78 | New backend for Steal An Egg (place 107778070777162): the walk-speed feature ported from the Blox Fruits player module, and only that -- a "Speed boost" toggle and a "Walk speed" slider (16-300, default 50) driven by a RenderStepped loop that re-applies the value every frame, so the game controller cannot overwrite it. Controls live in a fixed Speed tab.
0.0.77 | New backend for Blood Strike (place 114234929420007): a character ESP that reads fighters from workspace.Characters (Models named after their owner) with a fallback to the standard Players surface, plus a team check, range, and name/distance/health/chams options. Controls live in a fixed ESP tab.
0.0.76 | Every game now gets a fixed Dashboard tab built by the loader itself, so the same home screen shows no matter which backend runs: the player greeting card (avatar, name, clock) plus the game/server card, and live performance metrics (framerate, latency, players, session). It reuses the library's own dashboard builders, so there is no game-specific code.
0.0.75 | The shared UI theme was split into ten ordered modules under src/core/ui/theme/ (data, sub-tabs, constructor, theme-apply, notifications, window, extras, public-api, controls, lifecycle); the orchestrator loads them in the original section order and each adds its methods to the one Library table, so behaviour is unchanged while the 13k-line file is now maintainable in pieces.
0.0.74 | The real UI theme is now covered by an automated test: the suite loads the actual demon-theme.luau (not the stub the backend tests use), builds a window against a Roblox double, then creates a category, tab, section and controls and destroys it, so a theme regression fails the suite instead of only showing up in-game.
0.0.73 | The Debug tab and the print/warn log hooks are now opt-in and off by default, so the hub runs clean in every game; the collector and every backend probe keep running underneath. Set env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = true before loading, call __DEPHUB_DEBUG() live, or use the debug on|off console command to bring the tab back without reloading.
0.0.72 | The universal backend now builds its own UI in the shared shell (Movement, Visuals, Combate, Social, Servidor, Status) and instruments the Debug tab with its own probes, features and commands, so an unmapped game gets working controls and live diagnostics instead of an empty window.
0.0.71 | The loader now self-heals a stale bootstrap: when the remote version is newer than the cached build the executor served, it re-fetches DepHub.lua with a cache-bust token and re-executes it once, guarded against looping, so a fixed bootstrap URL never runs an old loader again.
0.0.70 | An unrecognised game now loads the universal backend automatically instead of stopping on the empty unsupported state; set env.__DEPHUB_DISABLE_UNIVERSAL to true to opt out and get the plain unsupported window back.
0.0.69 | The FPS metric now resolves through three routes in order -- FrameRate:GetValue(), FrameRate.Value, then the engine physics frame rate -- so it no longer sits at 0 on executors where FrameStats.FrameRate is exposed as a value object rather than a plain number.
0.0.68 | Module fetches now carry a per-session cache-bust token, so an executor's HttpGet cache can never serve a stale older revision of diagnostics.lua or any backend after main is updated. Also fixes the remote sniffer raising on an absent method and the Debug metrics crash when Stats members are missing.
0.0.67 | Remote sniffer no longer reads absent methods as raw indexes: a RemoteFunction has no FireServer and a RemoteEvent has no InvokeServer, so each method read is pcall-guarded and the absent one is skipped instead of raising and failing the whole backend load.
0.0.66 | Debug console metrics no longer crash when the executor does not expose Stats.FrameStats/Network: every optional Stats and Workspace read now resolves through FindFirstChild, so FPS/ping/titans degrade to no-data instead of raising.
0.0.65 | Every game backend now reports its own structure into the Debug tab: named instances, live feature state, watches and console commands, so a game update that breaks a dependency shows up as [FALHA] at the point of failure.
0.0.64 | Live Debug console: streaming log, live FPS/ping/memory/uptime metrics, feature state auto-refresh, warn/print/error hooks, remote sniffer and terminal commands in every game.
0.0.63 | Shared Debug tab: module health, game-structure probes, live feature state and a copyable diagnostic report in every game.
0.0.2 | Universal UI, lifecycle hardening and diagnostics
]],
    ["src/games/violence-district.lua"] = [[
local game, task, type, tostring, tonumber, pairs, ipairs, pcall, math, os = game, task, type, tostring, tonumber, pairs, ipairs, pcall, math, os
local Players = game:GetService("Players")
local RunService = game:GetService("RunService")
local Workspace = game:GetService("Workspace")
local ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService("ReplicatedStorage")

local LocalPlayer = Players.LocalPlayer
local env = type(getgenv) == "function" and getgenv() or _G
local STATE_KEY = "__DEPHUB_VIOLENCE_DISTRICT"

local previous = env[STATE_KEY]
if type(previous) == "table" and type(previous.Destroy) == "function" then
    pcall(previous.Destroy, previous)
end

if not LocalPlayer then
    return false
end

-- [CURE] Violence District backend.
--
-- Asymmetrical 1-vs-all horror: one Killer, the rest Survivors repairing
-- generators and escaping. Every mechanism below was confirmed against the live
-- game (see the notes the earlier reverse-engineering pass left behind), so the
-- features talk to the same objects the game itself uses:
--   * ESP         - Highlight + name billboard on each Killer/Survivor, read
--                   from Player.Team (Teams.Killer / Teams.Survivors).
--   * Auto Repair - fires Remotes.Generator.RepairEvent(point, true) at the
--                   nearest GeneratorPointN and stops when out of range.
--   * Auto Skill  - when the Skillcheck-gen object appears on the character,
--                   fires Remotes.Generator.SkillCheckResultEvent("success", ...).
--   * Auto Parry  - fires the Parrying Dagger's parry remote when the Killer
--                   plays a known parryable attack animation.
-- Remotes and the map are optional and resolved defensively: a missing one just
-- leaves that single feature idle instead of erroring the backend. Nothing here
-- hooks metatables or drives raw input; it only calls the game's own remotes.

local State = {
    Version = "1.0.0",
    Destroyed = false,
    Started = false,
    Connections = {},
    Toggles = {
        ESP = false,
        ESPKiller = true,
        ESPSurvivor = true,
        ESPNames = true,
        ESPDistance = true,
        Chams = true,
        AutoRepair = false,
        AutoSkillCheck = false,
        AutoParry = false,
        AutoEquip = true,
    },
    ToggleLabels = {
        ESP = "ESP enabled",
        ESPKiller = "Show Killer",
        ESPSurvivor = "Show Survivors",
        ESPNames = "Show name and role",
        ESPDistance = "Show distance",
        Chams = "Chams (outline)",
        AutoRepair = "Auto repair generator",
        AutoSkillCheck = "Auto skill check",
        AutoParry = "Auto Parry",
        AutoEquip = "Auto equip dagger",
    },
    Values = {
        Range = 2000,
        KillerColor = Color3.fromRGB(255, 74, 74),
        SurvivorColor = Color3.fromRGB(76, 188, 255),
        TextSize = 13,
        RepairRange = 14,
        ParryRange = 18,
        ParryCooldown = 1.0,
    },
    ESPObjects = {},
    RepairActive = false,
    RepairPoint = nil,
    RepairGenerator = nil,
}

env[STATE_KEY] = State
env.__DEPHUB = env.__DEPHUB or {}
env.__DEPHUB.ViolenceDistrict = State

local function track(connection)
    if connection then State.Connections[#State.Connections + 1] = connection end
    return connection
end

local function removed(object)
    return not object or object.Parent == nil
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Remotes are resolved lazily: a slow (or absent) remote must never block or
-- delay the panel building, so the tab is created first and resolveRemotes()
-- fills them in right after. Each stays nil-safe; the feature that needs it
-- simply does nothing while it is missing.
local RepairEvent, SkillCheckResult, ParryEvent

local function resolveRemotes()
    local remotes = ReplicatedStorage:FindFirstChild("Remotes")
    local generators = remotes and remotes:FindFirstChild("Generator")
    local items = remotes and remotes:FindFirstChild("Items")
    local dagger = items and items:FindFirstChild("Parrying Dagger")
    RepairEvent = (generators and generators:FindFirstChild("RepairEvent")) or RepairEvent
    SkillCheckResult = (generators and generators:FindFirstChild("SkillCheckResultEvent")) or SkillCheckResult
    ParryEvent = (dagger and dagger:FindFirstChild("parry")) or ParryEvent
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Shared helpers.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
local function roleOf(player)
    local team = player and player.Team
    local name = team and tostring(team.Name):lower() or ""
    if name:find("kill", 1, true) then return "Killer" end
    if name:find("surviv", 1, true) then return "Survivor" end
    return nil
end

local function roleColor(role)
    if role == "Killer" then return State.Values.KillerColor end
    return State.Values.SurvivorColor
end

local function characterRoot(character)
    if not character then return nil end
    return character:FindFirstChild("HumanoidRootPart")
        or character:FindFirstChild("RootPart")
end

local function headOf(character)
    if not character then return nil end
    return character:FindFirstChild("Head")
end

local function localCharacter()
    return LocalPlayer.Character
end

local function localRoot()
    local character = localCharacter()
    return character and (character:FindFirstChild("HumanoidRootPart") or character:FindFirstChild("Head"))
end

local function mapRoot()
    local map = Workspace:FindFirstChild("Map")
    if map then return map end
    for _, child in ipairs(Workspace:GetChildren()) do
        if child.Name == "Map" then return child end
    end
    return nil
end

local function buildLabel(character, role, distance)
    local parts = {}
    if State.Toggles.ESPNames then
        local player = Players:GetPlayerFromCharacter(character)
        local name = player and (player.DisplayName or player.Name) or character.Name
        parts[#parts + 1] = "[" .. role:upper() .. "] " .. name
    end
    if State.Toggles.ESPDistance and distance then parts[#parts + 1] = distance .. "m" end
    return table.concat(parts, " | ")
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ESP. Reconcile per frame: create records for what is visible and in range,
-- drop stale ones, repaint the rest. Role toggles, range and chams are all
-- re-evaluated live, so a slider or toggle takes effect without a relock.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
function State:RemoveESP(character)
    local record = self.ESPObjects[character]
    if not record then return end
    self.ESPObjects[character] = nil
    if record.Highlight then pcall(record.Highlight.Destroy, record.Highlight) end
    if record.Billboard then pcall(record.Billboard.Destroy, record.Billboard) end
end

function State:ClearESP()
    for character in pairs(self.ESPObjects) do
        self:RemoveESP(character)
    end
end

function State:CreateESP(character, role)
    self:RemoveESP(character)
    if self.Destroyed or not self.Toggles.ESP or removed(character) then return end
    local color = roleColor(role)

    local highlight = Instance.new("Highlight")
    highlight.Name = "DepHubVDHighlight"
    highlight.Adornee = character
    highlight.DepthMode = Enum.HighlightDepthMode.AlwaysOnTop
    highlight.FillColor = color
    highlight.FillTransparency = 0.42
    highlight.OutlineTransparency = 1
    highlight.Parent = character
    if not self.Toggles.Chams then highlight.Enabled = false end

    local billboard
    local label
    local head = headOf(character)
    if head then
        billboard = Instance.new("BillboardGui")
        billboard.Name = "DepHubVDName"
        billboard.Adornee = head
        billboard.AlwaysOnTop = true
        billboard.LightInfluence = 0
        billboard.Size = UDim2.fromOffset(220, 40)
        billboard.StudsOffsetWorldSpace = Vector3.new(0, 2.9, 0)
        billboard.Parent = head

        label = Instance.new("TextLabel")
        label.BackgroundTransparency = 1
        label.BorderSizePixel = 0
        label.Size = UDim2.fromScale(1, 1)
        label.Font = Enum.Font.GothamMedium
        label.TextColor3 = color
        label.TextStrokeTransparency = 1
        label.TextSize = self.Values.TextSize
        label.Text = buildLabel(character, role)
        label.Parent = billboard
    end

    self.ESPObjects[character] = {
        Highlight = highlight,
        Billboard = billboard,
        Label = label,
        Character = character,
        Role = role,
    }
end

function State:Refresh()
    if self.Destroyed or not self.Toggles.ESP then return end
    local me = localRoot()
    local seen = {}
    local range = self.Values.Range

    for _, player in ipairs(Players:GetPlayers()) do
        if player ~= LocalPlayer then
            local role = roleOf(player)
            local enabled = role == "Killer" and self.Toggles.ESPKiller
                or role == "Survivor" and self.Toggles.ESPSurvivor
            local character = player.Character
            if role and enabled and not removed(character) then
                local adornee = characterRoot(character) or headOf(character)
                local distance
                if me and adornee and adornee.Position then
                    distance = math.floor((adornee.Position - me.Position).Magnitude)
                end
                if not distance or distance <= range then
                    seen[character] = true
                    local record = self.ESPObjects[character]
                    if not record or record.Role ~= role then
                        self:CreateESP(character, role)
                        record = self.ESPObjects[character]
                    end
                    if record then
                        if record.Label then
                            record.Label.Text = buildLabel(character, role, distance)
                            record.Label.TextSize = self.Values.TextSize
                        end
                        if record.Highlight then record.Highlight.Enabled = self.Toggles.Chams end
                    end
                end
            end
        end
    end

    for character in pairs(self.ESPObjects) do
        if not seen[character] then self:RemoveESP(character) end
    end
end

function State:SetESP(enabled)
    self.Toggles.ESP = enabled == true
    if not self.Toggles.ESP then
        self:ClearESP()
        return true
    end
    self:Refresh()
    return true
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Auto Repair: fire RepairEvent(point, true) at the nearest GeneratorPointN
-- under the map and hold it until the point leaves range or the feature stops.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
local function nearestRepairPoint(maxRange)
    local root = localRoot()
    local map = mapRoot()
    if not root or not map then return nil, nil end
    local bestPoint, bestGenerator, bestDistance
    for _, descendant in ipairs(map:GetDescendants()) do
        if descendant.Name:match("^GeneratorPoint%d+$") then
            local part = descendant:IsA("BasePart") and descendant or descendant:FindFirstChildWhichIsA("BasePart")
            if part then
                local distance = (part.Position - root.Position).Magnitude
                if (not bestDistance or distance < bestDistance) and (not maxRange or distance <= maxRange) then
                    bestPoint, bestDistance = descendant, distance
                    local current = descendant.Parent
                    while current and current ~= map do
                        if current:IsA("Model") and current.Name:lower():find("generator", 1, true) then
                            bestGenerator = current
                            break
                        end
                        current = current.Parent
                    end
                end
            end
        end
    end
    return bestPoint, bestGenerator
end

function State:StopRepair(reason)
    if not self.RepairActive then return end
    local point = self.RepairPoint
    self.RepairActive = false
    self.RepairPoint = nil
    self.RepairGenerator = nil
    if point and point.Parent and RepairEvent then
        pcall(function() RepairEvent:FireServer(point, false) end)
    end
end

local function stepRepair()
    if State.Destroyed or not State.Toggles.AutoRepair then
        State:StopRepair()
        return
    end
    if State.RepairActive then
        local root = localRoot()
        local point = State.RepairPoint
        local part = point and point.Parent and (point:IsA("BasePart") and point or point:FindFirstChildWhichIsA("BasePart"))
        if not root or not part then
            State:StopRepair()
            return
        end
        if (part.Position - root.Position).Magnitude > State.Values.RepairRange + 2 then
            State:StopRepair()
        end
        return
    end
    if not RepairEvent then return end
    local point, generator = nearestRepairPoint(State.Values.RepairRange)
    if not point then return end
    State.RepairActive = true
    State.RepairPoint = point
    State.RepairGenerator = generator
    pcall(function() RepairEvent:FireServer(point, true) end)
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Auto Skill Check: the game spawns a Skillcheck-gen object on the character
-- while a check is up. Fire the success result remote at the generator we are
-- repairing, with an index read from the object (fallback 1).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
local lastSkillAt = 0

local function skillIndex(skillObject)
    if skillObject then
        for _, name in ipairs({"Index", "SkillCheckIndex", "SkillcheckIndex", "Check", "Number", "Stage"}) do
            local value = tonumber(skillObject:GetAttribute(name))
            if value then return value end
        end
    end
    return 1
end

local function submitSkillCheck()
    if State.Destroyed or not State.Toggles.AutoSkillCheck then return end
    if not SkillCheckResult then return end
    if os.clock() - lastSkillAt < 0.18 then return end

    local character = localCharacter()
    local skillTag = character and character:FindFirstChild("Skillcheck-gen")
    if not skillTag then return end

    local point, generator = State.RepairPoint, State.RepairGenerator
    if not (point and generator) then
        point, generator = nearestRepairPoint(math.max(State.Values.RepairRange, 24))
    end
    if not (point and generator) then return end

    lastSkillAt = os.clock()
    local index = skillIndex(skillTag)
    pcall(function() SkillCheckResult:FireServer("success", index, generator, point) end)
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Auto Parry: fire the Parrying Dagger's parry remote when the Killer plays a
-- known parryable attack animation inside range. Only animations that can be
-- parried belong here: charge/hold tells must not trigger it. The list is
-- intentionally short and documented -- only killer "veil" is confirmed so far,
-- so parry silently does nothing for a killer whose attack animation is not
-- listed. Add ids as they are confirmed in game.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
local PARRYABLE = {
    ["78935059863801"] = true, -- veil, basic attack
}

local parryReady = true

function State:TryParry()
    if self.Destroyed or not self.Toggles.AutoParry or not parryReady or not ParryEvent then return end
    local character = localCharacter()
    if not character then return end
    local dagger = character:FindFirstChild("Parrying Dagger")
        or (LocalPlayer.Backpack and LocalPlayer.Backpack:FindFirstChild("Parrying Dagger"))
    if not dagger then return end

    if self.Toggles.AutoEquip and dagger.Parent ~= character then
        local humanoid = character:FindFirstChildOfClass("Humanoid")
        if humanoid then pcall(function() humanoid:EquipTool(dagger) end) end
    end
    if not character:FindFirstChild("Parrying Dagger") then return end

    parryReady = false
    pcall(function() ParryEvent:FireServer() end)
    task.delay(self.Values.ParryCooldown, function() parryReady = true end)
end

local function watchKiller(character)
    if State.Destroyed or not character then return end
    local humanoid = character:FindFirstChildOfClass("Humanoid")
    local animator = humanoid and humanoid:FindFirstChild("Animator")
    if not animator then return end
    track(animator.AnimationPlayed:Connect(function(track_)
        if State.Destroyed or not State.Toggles.AutoParry then return end
        local id = track_ and track_.Animation and tostring(track_.Animation.AnimationId)
        if not id or not PARRYABLE[id:match("%d+")] then return end
        local hrp = localRoot()
        local khrp = characterRoot(character)
        if not hrp or not khrp then return end
        if (hrp.Position - khrp.Position).Magnitude > State.Values.ParryRange then return end
        State:TryParry()
    end))
end

local function watchPlayerForKiller(player)
    if player == LocalPlayer or not player then return end
    local function try(character)
        task.defer(function()
            if not State.Destroyed and roleOf(player) == "Killer" then watchKiller(character) end
        end)
    end
    if player.Character then try(player.Character) end
    track(player.CharacterAdded:Connect(try))
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Public setters
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
function State:SetToggle(name, enabled)
    if self.Destroyed or type(name) ~= "string" then return false end
    if self.Toggles[name] == nil then return false end
    self.Toggles[name] = enabled == true
    if name == "ESP" then
        return self:SetESP(enabled)
    elseif name == "ESPKiller" or name == "ESPSurvivor" or name == "Chams"
        or name == "ESPNames" or name == "ESPDistance" then
        if self.Toggles.ESP then self:Refresh() end
    elseif name == "AutoRepair" and not self.Toggles.AutoRepair then
        self:StopRepair()
    end
    return true
end

function State:GetToggle(name)
    if self.Destroyed or type(name) ~= "string" then return false end
    return self.Toggles[name] == true
end

function State:SetValue(name, value)
    if self.Destroyed or type(name) ~= "string" then return false end
    if self.Values[name] == nil then return false end
    value = tonumber(value)
    if not value or value ~= value then return false end
    self.Values[name] = value
    return true
end

function State:Start()
    if self.Started or self.Destroyed then return false end
    self.Started = true

    resolveRemotes()
    task.defer(function()
        if not self.Destroyed then resolveRemotes() end
    end)

    for _, player in ipairs(Players:GetPlayers()) do
        watchPlayerForKiller(player)
    end
    track(Players.PlayerAdded:Connect(watchPlayerForKiller))

    track(RunService.RenderStepped:Connect(function()
        if not self.Destroyed and self.Toggles.ESP then self:Refresh() end
    end))
    track(RunService.Heartbeat:Connect(function()
        if self.Destroyed then return end
        stepRepair()
        submitSkillCheck()
    end))
    return true
end

function State:Destroy()
    if self.Destroyed then return end
    self:StopRepair()
    self.Destroyed = true
    self:ClearESP()
    for _, connection in ipairs(self.Connections) do
        pcall(function() connection:Disconnect() end)
    end
    self.Connections = {}
    if env[STATE_KEY] == self then env[STATE_KEY] = nil end
    if env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.ViolenceDistrict == self then env.__DEPHUB.ViolenceDistrict = nil end
end

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Presentation: the shared shell. Tabs are grouped by CATEGORY (finalidade),
-- never by game name. This game uses only the categories it actually has:
--   Visual  - who to see on screen
--   World   - generator repair (a map object) and its skill-check minigame
--   Combat  - parry timing
-- The group -> tab -> section creation goes through the shared UI kit, so the
-- "column must be Left/Right" contract lives in one place.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
do
    local Kit = env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.UIKit
    if Kit then
        local visual = Kit.Section(Kit.Tab("Visual", "Visual"), "ESP", "Left")
        if visual then
            -- All seven ESP element switches are the same kind of decision, so they
            -- share lines three-up; the two value sliders keep their own rows.
            local espRow1 = Kit.Row(visual)
            if espRow1 then
                espRow1:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESP, State.Toggles.ESP, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESP", v) end)
                espRow1:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPKiller, State.Toggles.ESPKiller, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPKiller", v) end)
            else
                visual:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESP, State.Toggles.ESP, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESP", v) end)
                visual:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPKiller, State.Toggles.ESPKiller, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPKiller", v) end)
            end
            local espRow1b = Kit.Row(visual)
            if espRow1b then
                espRow1b:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPSurvivor, State.Toggles.ESPSurvivor, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPSurvivor", v) end)
                espRow1b:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.Chams, State.Toggles.Chams, function(v) State:SetToggle("Chams", v) end)
            else
                visual:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPSurvivor, State.Toggles.ESPSurvivor, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPSurvivor", v) end)
                visual:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.Chams, State.Toggles.Chams, function(v) State:SetToggle("Chams", v) end)
            end
            local espRow2 = Kit.Row(visual)
            if espRow2 then
                espRow2:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPNames, State.Toggles.ESPNames, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPNames", v) end)
                espRow2:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPDistance, State.Toggles.ESPDistance, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPDistance", v) end)
            else
                visual:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPNames, State.Toggles.ESPNames, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPNames", v) end)
                visual:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.ESPDistance, State.Toggles.ESPDistance, function(v) State:SetToggle("ESPDistance", v) end)
            end
            visual:CreateSlider("Max distance", 50, 5000, State.Values.Range, function(v) State:SetValue("Range", v) end)
            visual:CreateSlider("Text size", 8, 30, State.Values.TextSize, function(v) State:SetValue("TextSize", v) end)
        end

        local world = Kit.Section(Kit.Tab("Generators", "World"), "Repair and skill check", "Left")
        if world then
            -- Both switches drive the same generator helper, so they share a line.
            local genRow = Kit.Row(world)
            if genRow then
                genRow:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoRepair, State.Toggles.AutoRepair, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoRepair", v) end)
                genRow:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoSkillCheck, State.Toggles.AutoSkillCheck, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoSkillCheck", v) end)
            else
                world:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoRepair, State.Toggles.AutoRepair, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoRepair", v) end)
                world:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoSkillCheck, State.Toggles.AutoSkillCheck, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoSkillCheck", v) end)
            end
            world:CreateSlider("Repair range", 4, 40, State.Values.RepairRange, function(v) State:SetValue("RepairRange", v) end)
        end

        local combat = Kit.Section(Kit.Tab("Parry", "Combat"), "Auto parry", "Left")
        if combat then
            -- The two parry switches share a line; the two value sliders keep their rows.
            local parryRow = Kit.Row(combat)
            if parryRow then
                parryRow:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoParry, State.Toggles.AutoParry, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoParry", v) end)
                parryRow:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoEquip, State.Toggles.AutoEquip, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoEquip", v) end)
            else
                combat:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoParry, State.Toggles.AutoParry, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoParry", v) end)
                combat:CreateToggle(State.ToggleLabels.AutoEquip, State.Toggles.AutoEquip, function(v) State:SetToggle("AutoEquip", v) end)
            end
            combat:CreateSlider("Parry range", 5, 40, State.Values.ParryRange, function(v) State:SetValue("ParryRange", v) end)
            combat:CreateSlider("Parry cooldown", 0.5, 5, State.Values.ParryCooldown, function(v) State:SetValue("ParryCooldown", v) end)
        end
    end
end

State:Start()

do
    local Diag = env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.Diagnostics
    if Diag and type(Diag.RegisterModule) == "function" then
        Diag:RegisterModule("src/games/violence-district.lua")
        Diag:Feature("ViolenceDistrict.ESP", function() return State.Toggles.ESP and "ON" or "off" end)
        Diag:Feature("ViolenceDistrict.AutoRepair", function() return State.Toggles.AutoRepair and "ON" or "off" end)
        Diag:Feature("ViolenceDistrict.AutoParry", function() return State.Toggles.AutoParry and "ON" or "off" end)
        Diag:Watch("ViolenceDistrict alvos", function()
            local n = 0
            for _ in pairs(State.ESPObjects) do n = n + 1 end
            return n
        end)
        Diag:Command("vd", function()
            return "esp=" .. tostring(State.Toggles.ESP)
                .. " repair=" .. tostring(State.Toggles.AutoRepair)
                .. " parry=" .. tostring(State.Toggles.AutoParry)
                .. " repairRemote=" .. tostring(RepairEvent ~= nil)
                .. " parryRemote=" .. tostring(ParryEvent ~= nil)
        end)
    end
end

env.VD = State

return State

]],
    ["src/games/volleyball42.lua"] = [[
-- Volleyball 4.2 - esqueleto do backend. As features antigas (previsao da bola,
-- auto position, pulo forcado, ceiling noclip, Auto Set Aim, parts de set) foram
-- removidas para recomecar do zero. O que fica e so a AUTOMACAO DAS ACOES DO
-- JOGO: o modulo features/volleyball/actions.lua mapeia os remotes/animacoes
-- reais (set frontal/back, spike, block, bump, dive, spawn da bola, serve) e o
-- backend expoe isso como State:Act(nome, ...) para as features novas usarem.

local game, task, type, tostring, pcall = game, task, type, tostring, pcall
local Players = game:GetService("Players")

local LocalPlayer = Players.LocalPlayer
local env = type(getgenv) == "function" and getgenv() or _G
local STATE_KEY = "__DEPHUB_VOLLEYBALL42"
local BASE_URL = ((type(getgenv) == "function" and getgenv() or _G).__DEPHUB or {}).SourceBaseURL or "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/glowpkj/DepHub/main/"

-- Um reload limpo: derruba a sessao anterior (cancela serve pendente, etc.).
local previous = env[STATE_KEY]
if type(previous) == "table" and type(previous.Destroy) == "function" then
    pcall(previous.Destroy, previous)
end

if not LocalPlayer then
    return false
end

-- Carrega os remotes/animacoes de acao do jogo a partir do modulo de feature.
-- Nao bloquear o load se falhar: o backend ainda sobe, so as acoes ficam
-- indisponiveis (State:Act devolve false, "unavailable").
local function loadActions()
    local cacheBust = tostring((env.__DEPHUB or {}).SourceCacheBust or "")
    local okGet, source = pcall(function()
        return __dephubHttpGet(BASE_URL .. "src/games/features/volleyball/actions.lua" .. (cacheBust ~= "" and ("?cb=" .. cacheBust) or ""))
    end)
    if not okGet or type(source) ~= "string" or #source == 0 then return nil, tostring(source) end
    local okCompile, chunk, compileError = pcall(loadstring, source)
    if not okCompile or type(chunk) ~= "function" then return nil, tostring(compileError or chunk) end
    local okRun, module = pcall(chunk)
    if not okRun or type(module) ~= "table" or type(module.Set) ~= "function" then return nil, tostring(module) end
    return module
end

local Actions, actionsError = loadActions()
if not Actions then
    warn("[DepHub] Volleyball actions unavailable: " .. tostring(actionsError))
end

-- Carrega o modulo de Jump Anywhere + impulso (um unico recurso, um unico
-- toggle). Nao bloquear o load se falhar: sem ele o backend sobe e o toggle
-- reporta indisponivel.
local function loadFeature(relativePath, validate)
    local cacheBust = tostring((env.__DEPHUB or {}).SourceCacheBust or "")
    local okGet, source = pcall(function()
        return __dephubHttpGet(BASE_URL .. relativePath .. (cacheBust ~= "" and ("?cb=" .. cacheBust) or ""))
    end)
    if not okGet or type(source) ~= "string" or #source == 0 then return nil, tostring(source) end
    local okCompile, chunk, compileError = pcall(loadstring, source)
    if not okCompile or type(chunk) ~= "function" then return nil, tostring(compileError or chunk) end
    local okRun, module = pcall(chunk)
    if not okRun or type(module) ~= "table" or not validate(module) then return nil, tostring(module) end
    return module
end

local JumpAnywhere, jumpError = loadFeature("src/games/features/volleyball/jump-anywhere.lua", function(module)
    return type(module.new) == "function"
end)
if not JumpAnywhere then
    warn("[DepHub] Volleyball jump feature unavailable: " .. tostring(jumpError))
end

local State = {
    Version = "0.3.0",
    Destroyed = false,
    Started = false,
    Actions = Actions,
    ActionLog = {Actions = 0},
    Serving = false,
    ServeTask = nil,
    LastAnimation = nil,
    LastError = nil,
    -- Um unico recurso ligado a um unico toggle: jump bypass + impulso.
    Jump = nil,
    JumpEnabled = false,
    JumpImpulse = 25,
}

-- O modulo de acoes conta os disparos via actor.Log; aponta para o log do State.
State.Log = State.ActionLog

env[STATE_KEY] = State
env.__DEPHUB = env.__DEPHUB or {}
env.__DEPHUB.Volleyball42 = State

-- Superficie publica das acoes do jogo para as automacoes (e verificacao). O
-- ator e o proprio State: ele guarda a contagem de acoes, a task do serve e o
-- LastAnimation, entao o Destroy consegue cancelar um serve em andamento.
function State:Act(name, ...)
    if self.Destroyed then return false, "destroyed" end
    local action = self.Actions and self.Actions[name]
    if type(action) ~= "function" then return false, "unavailable" end
    local ok, result, err = pcall(action, self, ...)
    if not ok then
        self.LastError = tostring(result)
        return false, self.LastError
    end
    return result, err
end

function State:Start()
    if self.Started or self.Destroyed then return false end
    self.Started = true
    return true
end

-- === JUMP ANYWHERE (bypass + impulso) ===

-- Uma unica chave liga as duas partes: o bypass (pular em qualquer lugar) e o
-- impulso (acelerar o pulo). O slider so muda a intensidade do impulso; com 0%
-- fica so o bypass, com o pulo original.
--
-- Devolve true quando o IMPULSO ficou ligado -- ele nao depende do jogo. O
-- bypass e um extra: se nao puder ser aplicado (executor sem
-- debug.getupvalue/setupvalue, ou o modulo do jogo ausente), o impulso continua
-- valendo e o segundo retorno traz "bypass:<motivo>" para a UI avisar SEM dizer
-- que a feature inteira esta indisponivel. Nunca derruba o backend.
function State:SetJumpEnabled(enabled)
    if self.Destroyed then return false end
    local wanted = enabled == true

    if wanted then
        if not self.Jump then return false, "unavailable:feature" end
        if not self.JumpEnabled then
            self.Jump:SetImpulse(self.JumpImpulse)
            local bypassOk, bypassErr = self.Jump:Start()
            self.JumpEnabled = true
            if bypassOk ~= true then return true, "bypass:" .. tostring(bypassErr or "indisponivel") end
        end
    else
        if self.Jump and self.JumpEnabled then self.Jump:Stop() end
        self.JumpEnabled = false
    end
    return true
end

-- Impulso em % (0-150). Grava sempre; o efeito so vale com o toggle ligado.
function State:SetJumpImpulse(percent)
    if self.Destroyed then return false end
    local value = math.clamp(math.floor((tonumber(percent) or 0) + 0.5), 0, 150)
    self.JumpImpulse = value
    if self.Jump and self.JumpEnabled then self.Jump:SetImpulse(value) end
    return true
end

function State:Destroy()
    if self.Destroyed then return end
    self.Destroyed = true

    -- Desliga o recurso de pulo (restaura o upvalue original do jogo e desconecta).
    if self.Jump then
        pcall(self.Jump.Stop, self.Jump)
        self.Jump = nil
    end
    self.JumpEnabled = false

    -- Cancela um serve em andamento (ele tem um task.wait interno) para nao
    -- disparar "Serving" depois do unload.
    if self.Actions and type(self.Actions.Cancel) == "function" then
        pcall(self.Actions.Cancel, self)
    end

    if env[STATE_KEY] == self then
        env[STATE_KEY] = nil
    end
    if env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.Volleyball42 == self then
        env.__DEPHUB.Volleyball42 = nil
    end
end

-- Instancia o recurso de pulo (mecanismo pronto; so liga quando o toggle ligar).
if JumpAnywhere then
    State.Jump = JumpAnywhere.new()
end

State:Start()

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Apresentacao: uma aba Movement com uma secao e UM recurso. O toggle "Jump
-- Anywhere" liga o bypass + impulso juntos; o slider define a intensidade do
-- impulso. Cada um na sua linha: um SLIDER nao pode entrar num HStack estreito
-- (os internos dele sao ancorados a direita com offsets fixos, entao em meia
-- largura o label colapsa e os botoes -/+ vazam pra fora da borda).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
do
    local Kit = env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.UIKit
    if Kit then
        local tab = Kit.Tab("Movement")
        local section = Kit.Section(tab, "Jump", "Left")
        if section then
            section:CreateToggle("Jump Anywhere", State.JumpEnabled, function(value)
                local ok, err = State:SetJumpEnabled(value)
                if not ok then
                    State.LastError = tostring(err)
                    warn("[DepHub] Jump Anywhere unavailable: " .. tostring(err))
                elseif err then
                    -- O impulso esta ativo; so o bypass do jogo nao entrou.
                    State.LastError = tostring(err)
                    warn("[DepHub] Jump Anywhere: impulse on, jump bypass unavailable (" .. tostring(err) .. ")")
                end
            end)
            section:CreateSlider("Impulse", 0, 150, State.JumpImpulse, function(value)
                State:SetJumpImpulse(value)
            end)
        end
    end
end

return State

]],
    ["src/version.txt"] = [[
0.1.45

]],
}

-- Safe HttpGet resolver. Modules never call game:HttpGet directly: some
-- executors expose HttpGet only as a global, and writing a DataModel member
-- that does not exist errors. Resolve bundled paths from the in-memory table
-- first, then fall back to whatever fetch primitive the executor provides.
--
-- Published on every shared environment a dynamically-compiled module can
-- see: backends and features are compiled with loadstring and run outside
-- this chunk, so a chunk-local resolver would be invisible to them. On a real
-- executor getgenv(), _G and the chunk environment are the same table; in a
-- test harness they can differ, so all three are written.
local __dephubEnvs = {_G}
if type(getgenv) == "function" then
    local shared = getgenv()
    if type(shared) == "table" then __dephubEnvs[#__dephubEnvs + 1] = shared end
end
local globalHttpGet = (type(HttpGet) == "function") and HttpGet or nil
local function __dephubResolve(url)
    if type(url) ~= "string" then return nil end
    local clean = url:match("^[^?]*") or url
    local rel = clean:match("DepHub/[^/]+/(.+)$")
    if rel and type(BUNDLED_SOURCES[rel]) == "string" then return BUNDLED_SOURCES[rel] end
    for _, shared in ipairs(__dephubEnvs) do
        local table_ = shared.__DEPHUB_BUNDLED_SOURCES
        if type(table_) == "table" and rel and type(table_[rel]) == "string" then return table_[rel] end
    end
    if type(globalHttpGet) == "function" then return globalHttpGet(url) end
    local memberOk, member = pcall(function() return game.HttpGet end)
    if memberOk and type(member) == "function" then
        local ok, result = pcall(function() return game:HttpGet(url) end)
        if ok then return result end
    end
    return nil
end
local __dephubHttpGet = __dephubResolve
-- Publish to every shared environment so loadstring-compiled modules resolve
-- the rewritten call sites without a setfenv.
for _, shared in ipairs(__dephubEnvs) do
    shared.__dephubHttpGet = __dephubResolve
    shared.__DEPHUB_HTTP_GET = __dephubResolve
    shared.__DEPHUB_BUNDLED_SOURCES = BUNDLED_SOURCES
end


local sourceRef = tostring((...) or env.__DEPHUB_SOURCE_REF or "main")
-- Source hosts in fallback order. raw.githubusercontent.com is FIRST on purpose:
-- its edge cache is short-lived and it is the origin the project actually pushes
-- to, so it serves the current revision. jsDelivr re-resolves per request but its
-- per-file branch cache has repeatedly been caught serving an OLD revision (a
-- stale version.txt next to a fresh file, or vice-versa) -- exactly the failure
-- that made a run pick an old mirror and load a build missing every new feature.
-- It stays last as a fallback for when raw is unreachable. github.com/<ref>/raw/
-- is a third origin between them.
local HOST_TEMPLATES = {
    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/glowpkj/DepHub/" .. sourceRef .. "/",
    "https://github.com/glowpkj/DepHub/raw/" .. sourceRef .. "/",
    "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/glowpkj/DepHub@" .. sourceRef .. "/",
}
local BASE_URL = HOST_TEMPLATES[1]
local VERSION = "0.1.45"
local CACHE_KEY = "__DEPHUB_SOURCE_CACHE"
local EXECUTED_KEY = "__DEPHUB_LOADER_EXECUTED"
local STATE_KEY = "__DEPHUB_LOADER_STATE"

-- Executors cache game:HttpGet by exact URL, so a fixed source URL can keep
-- serving an older revision of a module even after main is updated. A token
-- that changes per session makes the module URLs unique, forcing a real fetch
-- of current source; GitHub's raw host ignores the query, so the file is the
-- same. The loader's own in-memory cache still keeps it to one fetch per module.
local CACHE_BUST = tostring(os.time()) .. "." .. tostring(math.floor((os.clock() % 1) * 1e6))

-- Identidade da sessao (nao e hash criptografico; e so um carimbo unico por
-- run, para o estado publicado em __DEPHUB ser rastreado ate uma execucao). O
-- ambiente do executor e compartilhado por varios scripts, entao todo campo que
-- uma sessao publica precisa saber de quem ele e -- e a sessao so limpa o que
-- lhe pertence (ver o bloco de publicacao abaixo).
local SESSION_ID = CACHE_BUST

local placeId = tostring(game.PlaceId)
local gameId = tostring(game.GameId)

-- Loader logging: the console only ever sees ONE line -- the DepHub version --
-- so a normal load is quiet. Everything else (progress, host resolution, the
-- exact failure) is still recorded for the Debug tab through Diagnostics.Log.
-- Genuine failures (a backend that threw, an unknown place) go to warn, so a
-- broken run is never silent.
local function log(message)
    local text = tostring(message)
    local diag = env and env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.Diagnostics
    if diag and type(diag.Log) == "function" then pcall(diag.Log, diag, "loader", text) end
end

-- A genuine failure is the one thing the console should show besides the
-- version: it goes to warn so a broken run is never silent.
local function report(message)
    local text = tostring(message)
    log(text)
    pcall(warn, "[DepHub] " .. text)
end

local function detectExecutor()
    local ok, name = pcall(function()
        if type(identifyexecutor) == "function" then return identifyexecutor() end
        if type(getexecutorname) == "function" then return getexecutorname() end
        return "Desconhecido"
    end)
    return ok and tostring(name) or "Desconhecido"
end

local BACKEND_KEYS = {
    "AOTR", "TSB", "MM2", "NoobPiece", "BloxFruits", "Universal", "Runtime", "Volleyball42",
    "BloodStrike", "StealAnEgg", "ViolenceDistrict", "StealAnExu"
}

local HANDLE_KEYS = {
    "Updater", "Window", "UIGuard", "TempUI", "UI", "DemonUI", "Scheduler", "HealthMonitor", "Dashboard", "AntiAFK"
}

local LEGACY_GLOBALS = {
    "__DEPHUB_UNIVERSAL", "__DEPHUB_TSB", "__DEPHUB_TSB_FRONTEND",
    "__DEPHUB_MM2", "__DEPHUB_AOTR", "__DEPHUB_MM2_FRONTEND",
    "__DEPHUB_AOTR_FRONTEND", "__DEPHUB_NOOBPIECE", "__DEPHUB_UI_GUARD",
    "__DEPHUB_DASHBOARD"
}

local function cleanupRuntime()
    local oldLoading = env.__DEPHUB_LOADING_INSTANCE
    if oldLoading then
        pcall(oldLoading.Destroy, oldLoading)
        env.__DEPHUB_LOADING_INSTANCE = nil
    end

    -- Release the previous diagnostics console first so its log hooks (warn/
    -- print interception) are removed before the new session installs its own.
    local previousDiag = env.__DEPHUB and env.__DEPHUB.Diagnostics
    if previousDiag and type(previousDiag.Destroy) == "function" then pcall(previousDiag.Destroy, previousDiag) end
    if env.__DEPHUB then env.__DEPHUB.Diagnostics = nil end

    env.__DEPHUB_UNIVERSAL = nil
    env.__DEPHUB_FRONTEND = nil
    env.__DEPHUB_UI_MODULES = nil
    env.__DEPHUB_UI_LOADING = nil

    local state = env.__DEPHUB
    if type(state) == "table" then
        for _, key in ipairs(BACKEND_KEYS) do
            local target = state[key]
            if type(target) == "table" and type(target.Destroy) == "function" then pcall(target.Destroy, target) end
            state[key] = nil
        end
        for _, key in ipairs(HANDLE_KEYS) do
            local target = state[key]
            if type(target) == "table" and type(target.Destroy) == "function" then pcall(target.Destroy, target) end
            state[key] = nil
        end
        state.Frontend = nil
        state.UILibrary = nil
        state.DemonUI = nil
        state.UI = nil
    end

    for _, key in ipairs(LEGACY_GLOBALS) do
        env[key] = nil
    end
end

local function sessionHealthy()
    local state = env.__DEPHUB
    if type(state) ~= "table" then return false end
    local backend = state.AOTR or state.TSB or state.MM2 or state.NoobPiece
        or state.BloxFruits or state.Universal or state.Runtime or state.Volleyball42
        or state.BloodStrike or state.StealAnEgg or state.ViolenceDistrict or state.StealAnExu
    return type(backend) == "table" and not backend.Destroyed
end

local previousState = env[STATE_KEY]
if type(previousState) == "table" and previousState.status == "running" then
    report("Initialization in progress. Wait before running again.")
    return false
end
if env[EXECUTED_KEY]
    and type(previousState) == "table"
    and previousState.status == "success"
    and previousState.Version == VERSION
    and previousState.PlaceId == placeId
    and previousState.GameId == gameId
    and previousState.SourceRef == sourceRef
    and sessionHealthy() then
    -- Já existe uma sessão viva desta mesma versão: nada a fazer. Logar deixa
    -- explicito, em vez de parecer que o loader morreu em silencio. O console
    -- recebe a mesma linha de versao de um load novo, para o run ser idempotente
    -- do ponto de vista de quem cola o bootstrap.
    log("Already running v" .. VERSION .. " (session healthy); skipping reload.")
    pcall(print, "DepHub v" .. VERSION)
    return true
end

cleanupRuntime()
env[EXECUTED_KEY] = false

-- The source-host resolver is defined once performRequest exists (below), but it
-- is referenced while publishing state, so it is forward-declared here.
local resolveBase

env[STATE_KEY] = {
    status = "running", startedAt = os.clock(), Version = VERSION,
    PlaceId = placeId, GameId = gameId, SourceRef = sourceRef
}

env.__DEPHUB = env.__DEPHUB or {}
env.__DEPHUB.Version = VERSION
-- Provodencial: o primeiro host candidato. httpGet refina isso via
-- resolveBase() no primeiro fetch de modulo e guarda o vencedor de volta, para
-- o valor que um modulo le depois ser o host que serviu a build atual.
-- So reusa o valor de sessao ANTERIOR se ele tiver sido guardado pela MESMA
-- ref; senao comeca da base candidata (a ref muda, a URL antiga nao vale mais).
if env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseRef ~= sourceRef then
    env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL = BASE_URL
end
env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL = env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL or BASE_URL
-- Publicado para cada backend montar sua URL de modulo unica por sessao e
-- evitar que um executor sirva uma revisao antiga em cache (ver CACHE_BUST).
env.__DEPHUB.SourceCacheBust = CACHE_BUST
-- A ref com que ESTA sessao foi lancada (o argumento branch/ref). Um backend se
-- re-executando (Auto execute via queue_on_teleport) le isso pra relancar a
-- MESMA build em vez de adivinhar. E escrito SEMPRE com a ref da sessao atual --
-- nao ha `or` preservando o valor antigo: se o usuario rodar outra ref no mesmo
-- ambiente, o estado compartilhado nao pode continuar apontando pra ref anterior
-- (era o bug em que o Auto execute reexecutava a versao velha).
-- SourceSession carimba de quem e o valor, para um limpar posterior nao
-- sobrescrever o de outra sessao.
env.__DEPHUB.SourceRef = sourceRef
env.__DEPHUB.SourceSession = SESSION_ID
env.__DEPHUB.Log = log
local session = env[STATE_KEY]

-- The diagnostics collector is declared here so early failures can be recorded,
-- but it is only instantiated after the module loader exists (below).
local Diag

env.__DEPHUB.Debug = function()
    return {
        Executor = detectExecutor(),
        RemoteVersion = env.__DEPHUB.RemoteVersion,
        SourceBaseURL = env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL,
        BaseVersion = env.__DEPHUB.BaseVersion,
        State = env[STATE_KEY],
        Cache = env[CACHE_KEY],
        Version = VERSION
    }
end

local function fail(reason)
    local message = tostring(reason or "Unknown failure")
    log("Failure: " .. message)
    pcall(warn, "[DepHub] Failure: " .. message)
    if Diag then Diag:Note("LOADER FAILURE: " .. message) end
    if env[STATE_KEY] ~= session then return false end
    cleanupRuntime()
    if env[STATE_KEY] == session then
        local state = env[STATE_KEY]
        if type(state) == "table" then
            state.status = "failed"
            state.error = message
            state.finishedAt = os.clock()
        end
        env[EXECUTED_KEY] = false
    end
    return false
end

local function getCache()
    local cache = env[CACHE_KEY]
    if type(cache) ~= "table" or cache.Version ~= VERSION or cache.SourceRef ~= sourceRef or type(cache.Sources) ~= "table" then
        cache = {Version = VERSION, SourceRef = sourceRef, Sources = {}}
        env[CACHE_KEY] = cache
    end
    return cache.Sources
end

local function performRequest(url)
    local requestFunctions = {}
    if type(request) == "function" then requestFunctions[#requestFunctions+1] = request end
    if type(http_request) == "function" then requestFunctions[#requestFunctions+1] = http_request end
    if type(syn) == "table" and type(syn.request) == "function" then requestFunctions[#requestFunctions+1] = syn.request end
    local lastError = "Resposta HTTP vazia"
    for attempt = 1, 2 do
        for _, requestFunction in ipairs(requestFunctions) do
            if requestFunction then
                local ok, response = pcall(requestFunction, {Url = url, Method = "GET"})
                if ok and type(response) == "table" then
                    local body = response.Body or response.body
                    local status = tonumber(response.StatusCode or response.status_code or 200) or 200
                    if status >= 200 and status < 400 and type(body) == "string" and #body > 0 then return true, body end
                    lastError = "HTTP " .. tostring(status)
                elseif not ok then
                    lastError = tostring(response)
                end
            end
        end
        if type(HttpGet) == "function" then
            local ok, result = pcall(HttpGet, url)
            if ok and type(result) == "string" and #result > 0 then return true, result end
            lastError = ok and "Resposta HTTP vazia" or tostring(result)
        end
        local memberOk, memberHttpGet = pcall(function() return game.HttpGet end)
        if memberOk and type(memberHttpGet) == "function" then
            local ok, result = pcall(function() return game:HttpGet(url) end)
            if ok and type(result) == "string" and #result > 0 then return true, result end
            lastError = ok and "Resposta HTTP vazia" or tostring(result)
        end
        if attempt < 2 then task.wait(0.1 + math.random() * 0.08) end
    end
    return false, lastError
end

-- Pick the source host that serves the current build. A host is accepted only
-- when its src/version.txt is EXACTLY this loader's version: a host that answers
-- version.txt but serves a stale tree is the failure that loaded a build missing
-- every new feature (jsDelivr kept a cached tree around the old version while
-- reporting it). Hosts are tried in HOST_TEMPLATES order -- raw first -- and the
-- winner is stored in env so later sessions and every module reuse one mirror.
resolveBase = function()
    if type(env.__DEPHUB) == "table" and type(env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL) == "string"
        and env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseRef == sourceRef then
        return env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL
    end

    local bestBase, bestVersion
    local exactBase
    local function segments(v)
        local out = {}
        for part in tostring(v):gmatch("%d+") do out[#out + 1] = tonumber(part) or 0 end
        return out
    end
    local function newer(a, b)
        if not a then return false end
        if not b then return true end
        local sa, sb = segments(a), segments(b)
        for i = 1, math.max(#sa, #sb) do
            local x, y = sa[i] or 0, sb[i] or 0
            if x ~= y then return x > y end
        end
        return false
    end

    for _, base in ipairs(HOST_TEMPLATES) do
        local token = tostring(os.time()) .. "." .. tostring(math.floor((os.clock() % 1) * 1e6))
        local ok, body = performRequest(base .. "src/version.txt?cb=" .. token)
        if ok and type(body) == "string" then
            local version = tostring(body):match("[%d%.]+")
            if version then
                -- Prefer a host serving EXACTLY this build: a host whose
                -- version.txt matches the loader is the one that also serves the
                -- current modules. A host ahead or behind is stale somewhere.
                if version == VERSION and not exactBase then
                    exactBase = base
                    break
                end
                if newer(version, bestVersion) then
                    bestBase, bestVersion = base, version
                end
            end
        end
    end

    local base = exactBase or bestBase or BASE_URL
    env.__DEPHUB = env.__DEPHUB or {}
    env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL = base
    env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseRef = sourceRef
    -- Report the version of the chosen host, not of whichever host happened to
    -- answer first: the log must match the mirror the modules are fetched from.
    env.__DEPHUB.BaseVersion = exactBase and VERSION or bestVersion
    return base
end

local function httpGet(path, useCache)
    local bundled = BUNDLED_SOURCES[path]
    if type(bundled) == "string" and #bundled > 0 then return true, bundled end
    local base = resolveBase()
    local cache = getCache()
    if useCache ~= false and type(cache[path]) == "string" and #cache[path] > 0 then return true, cache[path] end
    local separator = path:find("?", 1, true) and "&" or "?"
    local ok, result = performRequest(base .. path .. separator .. "cb=" .. CACHE_BUST)
    if not ok then return false, result end
    if useCache ~= false then cache[path] = result end
    return true, result
end

local function compile(source)
    if type(loadstring) ~= "function" then return false, "loadstring indisponivel" end
    local ok, chunk, compileError = pcall(loadstring, source)
    if not ok or type(chunk) ~= "function" then return false, tostring(compileError or chunk) end
    return true, chunk
end

local function loadModule(path, useCache)
    if env[STATE_KEY] ~= session then return false, "Sessao substituida" end
    local okSource, source = httpGet(path, useCache)
    if not okSource then return false, source end
    if env[STATE_KEY] ~= session then return false, "Sessao substituida" end
    local okCompile, chunk = compile(source)
    if not okCompile then return false, chunk end
    local okRun, result = pcall(chunk)
    if not okRun then return false, tostring(result) end
    return true, result
end

-- Instantiate the diagnostics collector now that the loader can fetch modules.
-- A no-op stand-in keeps every later Diag call valid even if it fails to load.
do
    local okDiag, diagModule = loadModule("src/core/diagnostics.lua", false)
    if okDiag and type(diagModule) == "table" and type(diagModule.new) == "function" then
        local okNew, instance = pcall(diagModule.new)
        if okNew and type(instance) == "table" then Diag = instance end
    end
    if not Diag then
        Diag = {SetEnvironment=function() end, RegisterModule=function() end, Module=function() end,
            Probe=function() end, Feature=function() end, Watch=function() end, Command=function() end,
            SniffRemote=function() end, RunCommand=function() return "" end, Note=function() end, Log=function() end,
            Install=function() return false end, Report=function() return "diagnostico indisponivel" end,
            LogText=function() return "" end, MetricsLine=function() return "" end, Destroy=function() end,
            Copy=function() return false, "diagnostico indisponivel" end}
        Diag.__stub = true
    end
    env.__DEPHUB.Diagnostics = Diag
end
Diag:RegisterModule("src/core/diagnostics.lua")
Diag:Module("src/core/diagnostics.lua", not Diag.__stub, Diag.__stub and "did not load" or "loaded")
Diag:SetEnvironment({
    Executor = detectExecutor(),
    Version = VERSION,
    PlaceId = placeId,
    GameId = gameId,
    GameName = tostring(game.Name or "?"),
    SourceRef = sourceRef,
    HttpGet = type(game.HttpGet) == "function" and "game:HttpGet" or "absent",
    request = type(request) == "function" and "present" or "absent",
    loadstring = type(loadstring) == "function" and "present" or "absent"
})

local executorName = detectExecutor()
log("Starting loader v" .. VERSION)
log("Executor detected: " .. tostring(executorName))
log("PlaceId: " .. placeId)
log("GameId: " .. gameId)

local okVersion, remoteVersion = httpGet("src/version.txt", false)
if okVersion then env.__DEPHUB.RemoteVersion = tostring(remoteVersion):match("[%d%.]+") or VERSION end
log("Source: " .. tostring(env.__DEPHUB.SourceBaseURL) .. " (remote " .. tostring(env.__DEPHUB.RemoteVersion or "?") .. ")")

-- Compare two dotted version strings numerically, segment by segment. Returns
-- true only when "a" is strictly newer than "b", so an older remote (a test
-- stub, a branch build, a stale mirror) never triggers a reload.
local function versionIsNewer(a, b)
    local function segments(v)
        local out = {}
        for part in tostring(v):gmatch("%d+") do out[#out + 1] = tonumber(part) or 0 end
        return out
    end
    local sa, sb = segments(a), segments(b)
    for i = 1, math.max(#sa, #sb) do
        local x, y = sa[i] or 0, sb[i] or 0
        if x ~= y then return x > y end
    end
    return false
end

-- Self-heal for the bootstrap cached by the executor. The loader's own module
-- fetches carry a per-session cache-bust token, but the bootstrap line a user
-- pastes (game:HttpGet(BASE_URL .. "DepHub.lua")) has a fixed URL, so an
-- executor can keep serving the old loader body even after main is bumped --
-- the running build then lags a whole version behind (old branch behaviour in
-- game). When the remote version is newer than this build, re-fetch the loader
-- itself with a fresh token and re-execute it. The guard records the version we
-- are switching to (in env, which survives the re-execution), so the freshly
-- loaded build sees the flag matching its own VERSION and does not loop.
local remV = env.__DEPHUB.RemoteVersion
if okVersion and type(remV) == "string" and versionIsNewer(remV, VERSION) and env.__DEPHUB_SELF_UPDATE_ATTEMPT ~= remV then
    env.__DEPHUB_SELF_UPDATE_ATTEMPT = remV
    log("Versao remota " .. remV .. " difere da local " .. VERSION .. "; recarregando loader.")
    local token = tostring(os.time()) .. "." .. tostring(math.floor((os.clock() % 1) * 1e6))
    local fetched, freshSource = performRequest(resolveBase() .. "DepHub.lua?cb=" .. token)
    if fetched and type(freshSource) == "string" and #freshSource > 0 then
        local okCompile, freshChunk = compile(freshSource)
        if okCompile then
            -- Retire this session before re-executing: the fresh build runs from
            -- the top and would otherwise see a "running" session and bail with
            -- "inicializacao em andamento". Clearing it lets the new build take
            -- over cleanly; this frame returns right after, so it never resumes.
            env[STATE_KEY] = nil
            local okRun, runError = pcall(freshChunk, sourceRef)
            if okRun then return true end
            report("Recarga do loader falhou ao executar: " .. tostring(runError))
        else
            report("Recarga do loader nao compilou: " .. tostring(freshChunk))
        end
    else
        report("Recarga do loader nao baixou: " .. tostring(freshSource))
    end
    -- Fall through: if the reload did not take, run the cached build as before
    -- rather than leaving the player with nothing. Restore the session that was
    -- cleared for the reload attempt so this frame can still finish cleanly.
    env[STATE_KEY] = session
end
-- This build is the one the guard was waiting for, so the attempt is complete.
if env.__DEPHUB_SELF_UPDATE_ATTEMPT == VERSION then env.__DEPHUB_SELF_UPDATE_ATTEMPT = nil end

local targets = {
    ["13379208636"] = {Core = "src/games/aotr.lua", AOTR = true},
    ["4658598196"] = {Core = "src/games/aotr.lua", AOTR = true},
    ["994732206"] = {Core = "src/games/bloxfruits.lua"},
    ["85211729168715"] = {Core = "src/games/bloxfruits.lua"},
    ["119048529960596"] = {Core = "src/games/rt3.lua"},
    ["10449761463"] = {Core = "src/games/tsb.lua", TSB = true},
    ["3808081382"] = {Core = "src/games/tsb.lua", TSB = true},
    ["142823291"] = {Core = "src/games/mm2.lua", MM2 = true},
    ["66654135"] = {Core = "src/games/mm2.lua", MM2 = true},
    ["84822469255086"] = {Core = "src/games/noobpiece.lua", NoobPiece = true},
    ["3840352284"] = {Core = "src/games/volleyball42.lua", Volleyball42 = true},
    ["1316421563"] = {Core = "src/games/volleyball42.lua", Volleyball42 = true},
    ["114234929420007"] = {Core = "src/games/bloodstrike.lua", BloodStrike = true},
    ["107778070777162"] = {Core = "src/games/steal-a-egg.lua", StealAnEgg = true},
    ["93978595733734"] = {Core = "src/games/violence-district.lua", ViolenceDistrict = true},
    ["6739698191"] = {Core = "src/games/violence-district.lua", ViolenceDistrict = true},
    ["103076400317371"] = {Core = "src/games/steal-an-exu.lua", StealAnExu = true},
    ["10771168447"] = {Core = "src/games/steal-an-exu.lua", StealAnExu = true}
}

local gameName = tostring(game.Name or ""):lower()
local isAOTRName = gameName:find("attack on titan") ~= nil and gameName:find("revolution") ~= nil
-- A known place/game id wins. The AOT:R name check is a documented fallback for
-- its newer universe. Any other game falls back to the universal backend, which
-- is built to run anywhere (movement, ESP, aim, chat, server hop) instead of
-- leaving the player with an empty window. Set env.__DEPHUB_DISABLE_UNIVERSAL
-- to true to opt out and get the plain "unsupported" state instead.
local knownTarget = targets[placeId] or targets[gameId] or (isAOTRName and {Core = "src/games/aotr.lua", AOTR = true}) or nil
if not knownTarget and env.__DEPHUB_DISABLE_UNIVERSAL ~= true then
    knownTarget = {Core = "src/games/universal.lua", Universal = true}
end
-- Nao usar um Wait() bloqueante no sinal de Loaded aqui: se o jogo ja carregou,
-- ele nao dispara de novo e o Wait travaria a thread para sempre (mesmo sintoma
-- do PlayerAdded removido). Se o jogo ainda estiver carregando, alguns frames de
-- espera ativa cobrem o caso sem prender o script indefinidamente.
for _ = 1, 30 do
    if game:IsLoaded() then break end
    task.wait(0.1)
end
task.wait()

-- The shell is identical for every game, so the loader only hands it the name
-- and version. Categories and tabs are the backend's job: each game fills the
-- shared shell with its own interface.
local okDemon, demonModule = loadModule("src/core/demon-ui.lua", false)
Diag:RegisterModule("src/core/demon-ui.lua")
if not okDemon or type(demonModule) ~= "table" or type(demonModule.new) ~= "function" then
    Diag:Module("src/core/demon-ui.lua", false, tostring(demonModule))
    return fail("The UI shell did not load: " .. tostring(demonModule))
end
Diag:Module("src/core/demon-ui.lua", true, "loaded")
log("UI module loaded; building window...")

local okWindow, window = pcall(demonModule.new, {
    Name = "DepHub",
    Version = VERSION
})
if not okWindow or type(window) ~= "table" then
    Diag:Module("demon-ui window", false, tostring(window))
    return fail("Could not build the UI window: " .. tostring(window))
end
Diag:Module("demon-ui window", true, "janela criada")
log("Window built; loading backend...")

env.__DEPHUB.DemonUI = window

env.__DEPHUB.UI = window

-- The UI kit every backend uses to build its part of the shell in the standard
-- shape (category -> tab -> section -> controls). Centralizing the category/tab/
-- section creation here is what keeps the "column must be Left/Right" contract
-- in one place instead of re-implemented (and mis-implemented) per game.
do
    local okKit, kitModule = loadModule("src/core/ui-kit.lua", false)
    if okKit and type(kitModule) == "table" then
        env.__DEPHUB.UIKit = kitModule
        Diag:Module("src/core/ui-kit.lua", true, "loaded")
    else
        Diag:Module("src/core/ui-kit.lua", false, tostring(kitModule))
    end
end

-- Fixed Dashboard tab: every game gets the same home screen, created here by
-- the loader so it exists no matter which backend (or no backend) runs. It
-- reuses the library's own dashboard builders -- CreateDashboardBanner (the
-- player greeting/avatar card plus the game/server card) and
-- CreatePerformanceDashboard (live framerate/latency/players/session metrics) --
-- so there is no game-specific code and no new UI to keep in sync. The builders
-- take the tab name, which the adapter returns on the tab record. Failures are
-- non-fatal: a dashboard that cannot be built must never stop the hub loading.
local function buildDashboardTab()
    if type(window.CreateTab) ~= "function" or type(window.Library) ~= "table" then return false end
    local ok, record = pcall(function() return window:CreateTab("Dashboard", "General", "barchart") end)
    if not ok or type(record) ~= "table" or type(record.Name) ~= "string" then
        Diag:Note("Dashboard: CreateTab falhou: " .. tostring(record))
        return false
    end
    local lib = window.Library
    local okBanner, bannerErr = pcall(function() lib:CreateDashboardBanner(record.Name) end)
    if not okBanner then Diag:Note("Dashboard: banner falhou: " .. tostring(bannerErr)) end
    local okPerf, perfErr = pcall(function() lib:CreatePerformanceDashboard(record.Name, "Right") end)
    if not okPerf then Diag:Note("Dashboard: metricas falharam: " .. tostring(perfErr)) end
    return okBanner and okPerf
end
local dashboardBuilt = buildDashboardTab()
Diag:Module("dashboard tab", dashboardBuilt, dashboardBuilt and "Dashboard tab created" or "could not build the Dashboard tab")

-- The Debug tab and the print/warn log hooks are opt-in. They are useful for
-- development but add a tab to every game and intercept every print, so by
-- default the hub runs clean. Set env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = true before
-- loading to get the tab, or call the console command `debug on` live. The
-- collector itself is always created, so backends keep instrumenting it and the
-- data is ready the moment the tab is shown -- nothing is thrown away.
local function debugEnabled()
    return env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG == true
end
if debugEnabled() then Diag:Install(window) end

-- Live switch: installs or removes the Debug tab and the log hooks without a
-- reload. Registered as a built-in console command on the collector.
Diag:Command("debug", function(argument)
    argument = tostring(argument or ""):lower()
    if argument == "on" or argument == "true" or argument == "1" then
        env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = true
        Diag:Install(window)
        return "debug ligado (aba Debug ativa)"
    elseif argument == "off" or argument == "false" or argument == "0" then
        env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = false
        Diag:Uninstall()
        return "debug desligado (aba Debug removida; coleta continua ativa)"
    end
    return "uso: debug on|off (atual: " .. tostring(debugEnabled()) .. ")"
end)

-- When the Debug tab is hidden there is no console to reach the command above,
-- so expose one global the user can call straight from the executor console to
-- bring it back: __DEPHUB_DEBUG() and __DEPHUB_DEBUG_OFF().
env.__DEPHUB_DEBUG = function()
    env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = true
    local ok = Diag:Install(window)
    return ok and "Debug tab active" or "could not activate the Debug tab"
end
env.__DEPHUB_DEBUG_OFF = function()
    env.__DEPHUB_ENABLE_DEBUG = false
    Diag:Uninstall()
    return "Debug tab removed"
end

-- Unknown game with universal disabled: keep the window, tell the player the
-- place is unsupported and stop. No incompatible backend is forced on them.
if not knownTarget then
    Diag:Note("Unknown game: no backend loaded (universal disabled).")
    pcall(function()
        local tab = window:CreateTab("Unsupported", "General", "shieldclose")
        if tab then
            local section = tab:CreateSection("State", "Left")
            if section then
                section:CreateParagraph("Unrecognized game",
                    "DepHub has no specific support for this place and the universal backend is disabled.\nPlaceId: " .. placeId ..
                    "\nGameId: " .. gameId .. "\nTo use the universal backend, set env.__DEPHUB_DISABLE_UNIVERSAL = false (or nil) and reload.")
            end
        end
    end)
    env.__DEPHUB.Mode = "Unsupported"
    env.__DEPHUB.Backend = nil
    env[EXECUTED_KEY] = true
    env[STATE_KEY].status = "unsupported"
    env[STATE_KEY].finishedAt = os.clock()
    report("Unknown game - PlaceId " .. placeId .. " / GameId " .. gameId)
    return true
end

local target = knownTarget
Diag:RegisterModule(target.Core)
log("Loading backend " .. tostring(target.Core) .. "...")
local okCore, coreResult = loadModule(target.Core, false)
if not okCore then
    Diag:Module(target.Core, false, tostring(coreResult))
    return fail("Core " .. tostring(target.Core) .. " failed: " .. tostring(coreResult))
end
local isRT3 = target.Core == "src/games/rt3.lua"
local isTSB = target.TSB == true
local isMM2 = target.MM2 == true
local isAOTR = target.AOTR == true
local isNoobPiece = target.NoobPiece == true
local isVolleyball42 = target.Volleyball42 == true
local isBloodStrike = target.BloodStrike == true
local isStealAnEgg = target.StealAnEgg == true
local isViolenceDistrict = target.ViolenceDistrict == true
local isStealAnExu = target.StealAnExu == true

if isRT3 then
    if coreResult ~= true then
        Diag:Module(target.Core, false, "retornou " .. tostring(coreResult))
        return fail("Modulo RT3 nao inicializou")
    end
    Diag:Module(target.Core, true, "loaded")
elseif type(coreResult) ~= "table" then
    Diag:Module(target.Core, false, "retornou " .. typeof(coreResult) .. " (" .. tostring(coreResult) .. ")")
    return fail("Core " .. tostring(target.Core) .. " retornou " .. typeof(coreResult) .. " (" .. tostring(coreResult) .. ")")
else
    Diag:Module(target.Core, true, "loaded")
end

env.__DEPHUB.Universal = target.Universal and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.BloxFruits = not target.Universal and not isRT3 and not isTSB and not isMM2 and not isAOTR and not isNoobPiece and not isVolleyball42 and not isBloodStrike and not isStealAnEgg and not isViolenceDistrict and not isStealAnExu and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.TSB = isTSB and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.MM2 = isMM2 and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.AOTR = isAOTR and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.NoobPiece = isNoobPiece and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.Volleyball42 = isVolleyball42 and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.BloodStrike = isBloodStrike and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.StealAnEgg = isStealAnEgg and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.ViolenceDistrict = isViolenceDistrict and coreResult or nil
env.__DEPHUB.StealAnExu = isStealAnExu and coreResult or nil

local backend
if target.Universal then backend = coreResult
elseif isRT3 then backend = env.__DEPHUB.Runtime
elseif isTSB then backend = coreResult
elseif isMM2 then backend = coreResult
elseif isAOTR then backend = coreResult
elseif isNoobPiece then backend = coreResult
elseif isVolleyball42 then backend = coreResult
elseif isBloodStrike then backend = coreResult
elseif isStealAnEgg then backend = coreResult
elseif isViolenceDistrict then backend = coreResult
elseif isStealAnExu then backend = coreResult
else backend = coreResult end

-- Informational only: the shell no longer changes per game, so this label
-- simply names which backend was loaded.
env.__DEPHUB.Mode = tostring(knownTarget.Core or "Game"):match("([^/]+)%.lua$") or "Game"
env.__DEPHUB.Backend = backend

if env[STATE_KEY] ~= session then return false end

env[EXECUTED_KEY] = true
env[STATE_KEY].status = "success"
env[STATE_KEY].finishedAt = os.clock()
-- The one line the console sees on a healthy load.
log("Backend loaded. DepHub v" .. VERSION .. " ready.")

print("DepHub v" .. VERSION)

task.defer(function()
    if env[STATE_KEY] ~= session or session.status ~= "success" then return end
    local ok, updaterModule = loadModule("src/core/updater.lua", false)
    if not ok or type(updaterModule) ~= "table" or type(updaterModule.new) ~= "function" then return end
    local okNew, updater = pcall(function()
        return updaterModule.new({PlaceId = game.PlaceId, GameId = game.GameId, PollInterval = 30, Mode = "serverhop"})
    end)
    if okNew and updater then
        if env[STATE_KEY] ~= session then
            if type(updater.Destroy) == "function" then updater:Destroy() end
            return
        end
        env.__DEPHUB.Updater = updater
        pcall(updater.Start, updater)
    end
end)

return true
